===1930s===
{{main|King Kong (1933 film)|Son of Kong}}
In 1933, [[RKO Pictures]] produced and released ''[[King Kong (1933 film)|King Kong]]''; it became a box office success despite opening during the [[Great Depression]], earning $90,000 in its first four days.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://time.com/3720513/king-kong-history/|title=How King Kong Gripped Audiences, Despite Ham-Handed Effects|first=Jennifer|last=Latson|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|date=March 2, 2015|access-date=April 21, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422041639/https://time.com/3720513/king-kong-history/}}</ref> The film's success spawned a sequel nine months later, ''[[Son of Kong]]'', as well as what became a multimedia franchise, spanning remakes, reboots, books, videos games, attractions, parodies and references.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodinsider.com/king-kong-1933-success/|title=''King Kong'': The 1933 Original, Still Mighty Decades Later|first=Austin|last=Oguri|work=Hollywood Insider|date=February 22, 2022|access-date=April 21, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422042817/https://www.hollywoodinsider.com/king-kong-1933-success/}}</ref><ref name="THR Haar">{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/lists/king-kong-years-how-giant-gorilla-has-evolved-1933-982360/king-kong-1933/|title=King Kong Through the Years: How the Giant Gorilla Has Evolved Since 1933|first=Kara|last=Haar|work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|date=March 9, 2017|access-date=April 21, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dw.com/en/king-kong-a-brief-history-of-a-90-year-old-movie-icon/a-64854235|title=King Kong: A brief history of a 90-year-old movie icon|first=Philipp|last=Jedicke|work=DW|date=March 2, 2023|access-date=April 21, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422045532/https://www.dw.com/en/king-kong-a-brief-history-of-a-90-year-old-movie-icon/a-64854235}}</ref>

The film grossed $10 million at the box office and earned an additional $2.5 million when it was re-released in 1952.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.insider.com/most-popular-movie-every-year-2017-8#1933-king-kong-3|title=The biggest box-office movie that came out the year you were born|first1=Jason|last1=Guerrasio|first2=Jacob|last2=Shamsian|first3=Tanza|last3=Loudenback|work=Insider|date=August 27, 2020|access-date=April 21, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20230422050446/https://www.insider.com/most-popular-movie-every-year-2017-8#1933-king-kong-3}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.life.com/arts-entertainment/king-kong-screenings-eisenstaedt/|title=King Kong: When the Awesome One Showed His Might|first1=Lily|last1=Rothman|first2=Liz|last2=Ronk|magazine=Life|date=March 10, 2017|access-date=April 21, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422050102/https://www.life.com/arts-entertainment/king-kong-screenings-eisenstaedt/}}</ref> The film revolutionized special effects, specifically [[stop motion]], that left a long lasting impact in the film industry worldwide, with effects animator [[Willis H. O'Brien]] largely credited for pioneering the medium.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/film/1999/jun/18/features1|title=The Kingmaker|first=Betsy|last=Reed|work=The Guardian|date=June 18, 1999|access-date=April 21, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422044313/https://www.theguardian.com/film/1999/jun/18/features1}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://time.com/4696931/king-kong-franchise-special-effects/|title=5 Photos That Show How King Kong Revolutionized Movie Special Effects|first=Eliza|last=Berman|magazine=Time|date=March 9, 2017|access-date=April 21, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422043820/https://time.com/4696931/king-kong-franchise-special-effects/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://filmschoolrejects.com/king-clay-willis-h-obriens-stop-motion-reality/|title=The King of Clay: Willis H. O'Brien's Stop-Motion Reality|first=Jacob|last=Oller|work=Film School Rejects|date=October 2, 2017|access-date=April 21, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422044621/https://filmschoolrejects.com/king-clay-willis-h-obriens-stop-motion-reality/}}</ref> ''King Kong'' (1933) is widely regarded by critics and journalists as a masterpiece and a signature facet of [[Cinema of the United States|American cinema]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.empireonline.com/movies/reviews/king-kong-legend-reborn-review/|title=King Kong: The Legend Reborn Review|first=Kim|last=Newman|work=[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]]|date=March 1, 2006|access-date=April 26, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 27, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230427022059/https://www.empireonline.com/movies/reviews/king-kong-legend-reborn-review/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2020/03/15/why-original-1933-king-kong-still-best-by-far/5052762002/|title=Here's why the original 'King Kong' is still one of the greatest films of all time|first=Ed|last=Symkus|work=[[USA Today]]|date=March 15, 2020|access-date=April 26, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 27, 2023|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20230427020455/https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2020/03/15/why-original-1933-king-kong-still-best-by-far/5052762002/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/king-kong-peter-jackson-bring-horror-to-blockbuster-cinema/|title=King Kong: When Peter Jackson Tried to Bring Horror to Blockbuster Cinema|first=Chris|last=Farnell|work=Den of Geek|date=January 19, 2023|access-date=April 26, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 27, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230427022510/https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/king-kong-peter-jackson-bring-horror-to-blockbuster-cinema/}}</ref> and is cited as one of the greatest monster films ever made.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://entertainment.time.com/2005/02/12/all-time-100-movies/slide/king-kong-1933/|title=King Kong – Best Movies of All Times|first=Richard|last=Schickel|magazine=Time|date=January 14, 2010|access-date=April 26, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 27, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230427022852/https://entertainment.time.com/2005/02/12/all-time-100-movies/slide/king-kong-1933/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/jamos/2020/03/02/back-on-cinema-screens-after-65-years-why-king-kong-remains-the-greatest-movie-spectacle-of-all-time/?sh=6321ea33dec3|title=Back On Cinema Screens After 65 Years, Why ''King Kong'' Remains the Greatest Movie Spectacle of All-Time|first=Jim|last=Amos|work=Forbes|date=March 2, 2020|access-date=April 26, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20230301-king-kong-at-90-the-greatest-monster-film-ever-made|title=King Kong at 90: The greatest monster film ever made|first=Nicholas|last=Barber|work=[[BBC News]]|date=March 1, 2023|access-date=April 26, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 27, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230427023535/https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20230301-king-kong-at-90-the-greatest-monster-film-ever-made}}</ref> ''King Kong'' (1933) was selected by the [[National Film Registry]] for preservation.<ref name="THR Haar"/>

===1960s===
{{main|King Kong vs. Godzilla|The King Kong Show|King Kong Escapes}}
In the early 1960s, O'Brien developed a story outline titled ''King Kong vs. Frankenstein'', supplemented with water-color illustrations and sketches, in the hopes of igniting interest for a potential production in color. Impressed with O'Brien's pitch, RKO attorney Daniel O'Shea granted O'Brien permission to use the character and introduced him to independent film producer [[John Beck (producer)|John Beck]] to help secure studio funding. Despite hiring [[George Worthing Yates]] to adapt O'Brien's outline into a full screenplay (retitled as ''King Kong vs. Prometheus'', in reference to the [[Frankenstein|original Mary Shelley book]]) and meeting with several studios and directors, Beck failed to sell the project in Hollywood and reached out to foreign studios.{{sfn|Ryfle|1998|pp=80–81}}

Beck succeeded in striking a deal with [[Toho|Toho Co., Ltd.]], the producers behind ''[[Godzilla (1954 film)|Godzilla]]'' (1954). However, Toho was mostly interested in licensing King Kong from RKO and Beck rather than acquiring O'Brien's story. As a result, Frankenstein/Prometheus was replaced with [[Godzilla]] and the project was redeveloped as ''[[King Kong vs. Godzilla]]''. Beck's deal with Toho was done without O'Brien's knowledge or consent. His representative prepared a lawsuit (presumably against Beck) for unauthorized use of O'Brien's ideas in the film. However, the lawsuit was dropped due to attorney fees, and O'Brien died on November 8, 1962.{{sfn|Ryfle|1998|pp=80–81}}

Unlike ''King Kong'' (1933), the special effects in ''Godzilla'' were accomplished with [[suitmation]] and miniature sets, also known as ''[[tokusatsu]]''. King Kong was portrayed by [[Shoichi Hirose]] and Godzilla portrayed by [[Haruo Nakajima]]; stop motion was also used in a few brief scenes.{{sfn|Ryfle|1998|p=84}}

Toho theatrically released ''King Kong vs. Godzilla'' on August 11, 1962, in Japan and it was a box office success, having sold 11.2 million tickets during its initial theatrical run. It earned {{¥|352 million}} ($972,000) in [[Theatrical rental|distribution rental]] earnings.{{sfn|Ryfle|Godziszewski|2017|p=191}} The film became the [[List of highest-grossing Japanese films|second-highest-grossing Japanese-produced film in history]] upon its release{{sfn|Nakamura|Shiraishi|Aita|Tomoi|2014|p=162}} and was the fourth-highest-grossing film released in Japan that year, as well as Toho's second-biggest release.{{sfn|Galbraith IV|2008|p=194}}

Because Toho was so eager to acquire King Kong, they had agreed to let Beck retain exclusive distribution rights (theatrical and television) for the United States, Canada, Alaska, the United Kingdom, and Israel, while Toho retained exclusive distribution for the Far East. Beck's contract also granted him the right to produce his own version. Beck commissioned a heavily localized version with new footage using American actors and replacing [[Akira Ifukube]]'s score with stock music from [[Universal Pictures]]' library. Beck sold his localized version to [[Universal Pictures#Universal-International and Decca Records|Universal International]], who made a deal to retain copyrights to the American version for 40 years.{{sfn|Ryfle|1998|p=87}}

The American version of ''King Kong vs. Godzilla'' was released in the United States on June 26, 1963.{{sfn|Ryfle|1998|p=79}} The Japanese version remained unavailable officially outside of Japan until 2019, when American distributor [[The Criterion Collection]] included both Japanese and American versions in a Blu-ray set collecting the [[Godzilla (franchise)#Shōwa era (1954–1975)|Shōwa era ''Godzilla'']] films.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.polygon.com/2019/7/25/8930381/godzilla-criterion-collection-showa-era-films-release-date|title=Criterion reveals the collection's 1000th disc: the ultimate Godzilla set|last=Patches|first=Matt|date=July 25, 2019|website=[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]]|access-date=July 25, 2019|url-status=live|archive-date=December 17, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191217223541/https://www.polygon.com/2019/7/25/8930381/godzilla-criterion-collection-showa-era-films-release-date}}</ref>

In 1966, American studio [[Rankin/Bass Animated Entertainment]] acquired the rights from RKO to produce an animated series and a new live-action film. A joint venture between Rankin/Bass and Japanese studio [[Toei Animation]], ''[[The King Kong Show]]'' premiered on [[American Broadcasting Company|ABC]] in September 1966.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.scifijapan.com/godzilla-toho/king-kong-cartoon-series-guide|title=King Kong Cartoon Series Guide|first=Keith|last=Aiken|work=SciFi Japan|date=December 20, 2008|access-date=April 22, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422164611/https://www.scifijapan.com/godzilla-toho/king-kong-cartoon-series-guide}}</ref> Around that time, Rankin/Bass made a deal with Toho to co-produced their King Kong film that would have been promotionally tied to ''The King Kong Show''. Toho presented a script titled ''Operation Robinson Crusoe: King Kong vs. Ebirah'', but Rankin/Bass vetoed the script and Toho proceeded without Rankin/Bass; King Kong was replaced with Godzilla and the film was redeveloped as ''[[Ebirah, Horror of the Deep]]''.{{sfn|Ryfle|1998|p=135}}{{sfn|Ryfle|Godziszewski|2017|p=239}}

In September 1966, Toho had sent Rankin/Bass a proposed synopsis for a new film titled ''[[King Kong Escapes]]'', which was loosely based on ''The King Kong Show''; though some elements from ''Operation Robinson Crusoe: King Kong vs. Ebirah'' remained such as over-the-top villains from an unnamed nation, illegal production of fuel for nuclear weapons, and scenes in the South Seas. The proposal was accepted and filming began in spring 1967,{{sfn|Ryfle|Godziszewski|2017|p=239}} with Haruo Nakajima playing King Kong via suitmation.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2017/08/haruo-nakajima-who-wore-godzilla-suit-dies-at-88-1202144207/|title=Haruo Nakajima, the Man Inside the Godzilla Suit, Dies at 88|first=Dade|last=Hayes|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=August 7, 2017|access-date=April 22, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 22, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422073603/https://deadline.com/2017/08/haruo-nakajima-who-wore-godzilla-suit-dies-at-88-1202144207/}}</ref> Toho theatrically released ''King Kong Escapes'' on July 22, 1967, in Japan but failed to match the box office success of ''King Kong vs. Godzilla''.{{sfn|Ryfle|Godziszewski|2017|p=243}} Universal Pictures released an English dubbed version in the United States the following year; similar to what they had done with ''King Kong vs. Godzilla'', Universal made a deal to retain the copyright of the American version for 40 years.{{sfn|Ryfle|1998|p=87}}

===1970s–1980s===
{{main|King Kong (1976 film)|King Kong Lives}}
Accounts vary as to the genesis of the [[King Kong (1976 film)|1976 remake]]. Producer [[Dino De Laurentiis]] has claimed it was his idea to remake ''King Kong'' after seeing a poster of the 1933 film in his daughter's room. [[Michael Eisner]], then-vice president of [[American Broadcasting Company|ABC]], equally claimed to have conceived the idea of a remake in December 1974 after watching the 1933 film on television, and casually mentioned the idea to [[Sidney Sheinberg]], then-president and CEO of [[MCA Inc.]] and [[Universal Pictures]], and Sheinberg also mentioned the idea to [[Barry Diller]], then-president of [[Paramount Pictures]]. Sheinberg and Diller soon began developing their own ''King Kong'' remakes simultaneously, both unaware of the other's project. Diller hired De Laurentiis to produce on behalf of Paramount, while Sheinberg hired [[Hunt Stromberg Jr.]] to produce for Universal. On April 15, 1975, Universal's attorney Arnold Shane and De Laurentiis met with RKO's attorney Daniel O'Shea to negotiate in acquiring the rights; Shane and De Laurentiis were unaware of each other's appointment. Despite not signing documents, both parties left believing they had secured the rights for their respected studios. However, Paramount had signed their deal with RKO the following month and soon learned about Universal's own film after they announced [[Joseph Sargent]] to direct and [[Bo Goldman]] to write the script, titled ''The Legend of King Kong''.<ref name="DOG Kong">{{cite web|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/the-struggles-of-king-kong-76/|title=The Struggles of King Kong '76|last=Lambie|first=Ryan|work=Den of Geek|date=March 10, 2017|access-date=March 28, 2017|url-status=live|archive-date=April 27, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230427001355/https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/the-struggles-of-king-kong-76/}}</ref>

Universal and Paramount soon engaged in litigation. Universal insisted that a verbal agreement was made with RKO, demanded $25 million in damages from De Laurentiis and RKO, and asserted that the material was in public domain by then since Universal published a novelization of the ''King Kong'' story in the 1930s. De Laurentiis countersued for copyright infringement, demanding $90 million in damages and pushed his film into production while also publishing ads that jabbed at Universal (e.g. "There Still is Only One King Kong"). In January 1976, negotiations were made to potentially have both studios co-produce a single film and share profits, but De Laurentiis rejected Universal's proposal of using their script and controlling the merchandise. Litigation ended that same month after De Laurentiis agreed to pay an undisclosed percentage for Universal's cancelled film, with Universal vowing to produce their own ''King Kong'' film at an undetermined date.<ref name="DOG Kong"/>

[[Carlo Rambaldi]] lead an effects team that built Kong's giant hand and a 40 foot tall robot of Kong, its construction overseen by [[Glen Robinson (visual effects)|Glen Robinson]]. Like the Toho films, an ape-suit was used to bring Kong to life, with special make-up effects artist [[Rick Baker]] portraying the character. The 40-foot robot cost roughly $2 million, while the giant hand and suit cost an additional $400,000, and a full-sized static Kong made of styrofoam, used for one scene, cost $300,000. The production was a rushed venture since the studio set the film's release date for December 1976 and De Laurentiis' persistence to beat Universal. As a result, delays occurred due to props and mechanics malfunctioning; two full-scale right hands produced by mistake; director [[John Guillermin]]'s tyrant-like behavior on-set; the budget inflating to $24 million and marketing costs of $10 million, figures unheard of at the time. Paramount theatrically released ''King Kong'' on December 17, 1976, and grossed $90 million. Despite mixed reviews unfavorably comparing the remake to the original, the film won an [[Academy Award for Best Visual Effects#1970s|Academy Award for Best Visual Effects]], shared with ''[[Logan's Run (film)|Logan's Run]]''.<ref name="DOG Kong"/>

In 1977, De Laurentiis stated that a sequel to the 1976 remake would "definitely" be produced.<ref>THE PRODUCER TALKS ABOUT HIS NEW "KING KONG" Anonymous. American Cinematographer; Hollywood Vol. 58, Iss. 1, (Jan 1977): 40-42.</ref> However, development on a sequel was impaired by the remake's disappointing box office results and legal complications over the ownership of the King Kong character.<ref>'Kong' Battle Advances By ALJEAN HARMETZ. New York Times 27 Jan 1978: C20.</ref> Writer [[Steven Pressfield]] stated that various scripts were written that he described as "embarrassing". Despite initially skeptical of a female Kong, De Laurentiis ultimately approved of Pressfield's and [[Ronald Shusett]] pitch of Kong put in a respirator and restored with an artificial heart.<ref name="fang">{{cite magazine|magazine=Fangoria|first=Edward|last=Gross|title=King Kong Lives|url=https://archive.org/details/Fangoria_058_c2c_1986_Fly_2_Sprout-DREGS/page/n10/mode/1up?q=%22king+kong+lives%22|page=10|issue=58|date=October 1986}}</ref> Guillermin returned to direct the sequel after finances collapsed for a film adaptation of ''[[Tai-Pan (novel)|Tai-Pan]]'' with [[Sean Connery]].<ref name="star">{{cite magazine|magazine=Starlog|title=Lord of Disaster|first=Lowell|last=Goldman|date=November 1990|page=70|url=https://archive.org/details/starlog_magazine-160/page/n69/mode/1up?q=toreadors+guillermin}}</ref> The film was announced in October 1985 and filming began in April 1986 in Tennessee for a December release later that year.<ref>A DYLAN THOMAS SCRIPT, BUT, ALAS, LITTLE ELSE Ryan, Desmond. Philadelphia Inquirer 27 Oct 1985: I.2.</ref><ref>GIANT ROLE Orlando Sentinel 29 Mar 1986: A2.</ref> ''[[King Kong Lives]]'' was theatrically released on December 19, 1986, by [[De Laurentiis Entertainment Group]] and was met with a poor response. The film flopped at the box office,<ref>{{cite news|title= 'Kong Lives' Dies At Box Office|work= [[Los Angeles Times]]|date=December 24, 1986|url= https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1986-12-24-ca-64-story.html|access-date=April 26, 2023|first=Jack|last=Mathews}}</ref> grossing $4.7 million in the United States and Canada,<ref>{{cite Box Office Mojo|id=0091344 |title=King Kong Lives (1986) |accessdate=April 26, 2023}}</ref> and was panned by critics.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://ultimateclassicrock.com/king-kong-lives-movie/|title=35 Years Ago: Why ''King Kong Lives'' Died a Quick Box Office Death|first=Tyler|last=Sage|work=Ultimate Classic Rock and Culture|date=December 19, 2021|access-date=April 26, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 27, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230427020628/https://ultimateclassicrock.com/king-kong-lives-movie/}}</ref>

===1990s===
{{main|King Kong (2005 film)#Earlier attempts and 1990s|The Mighty Kong}}
In 1993, Toho had considered remaking ''King Kong vs. Godzilla'', due to that film being their most successful Godzilla/Kong film to date. However, Toho concluded that re-acquiring the rights to King Kong would prove difficult and instead considered using the cyborg Mechani-Kong, seen in ''The King Kong Show'' and Toho's ''King Kong Escapes''. Effects director [[Kōichi Kawakita]] regaled an idea inspired by ''[[Fantastic Voyage]]'' where Mechani-Kong would have injected people inside Godzilla's body to battle the beast internally while Mechani-Kong battled Godzilla externally. Kawakita stated that the concept would have explored different worlds inside Godzilla. However, Toho walked back on using Mechani-Kong since the character bore a likeness to King Kong and thus required obtaining the rights. Mechani-Kong was replaced with [[Mechagodzilla]] and the film was redeveloped as ''[[Godzilla vs. Mechagodzilla II]]''.{{sfn|Ryfle|1998|p=293}}

In 1995, Universal Pictures offered filmmaker [[Peter Jackson]] a chance to direct a remake of ''King Kong'', after being impressed with Jackson's ''[[The Frighteners]]''.{{sfn|Morton|2005|pp=316–329}} Licensing the character from RKO and Time Warner was no longer an issue because the character of "Kong" fell into public domain by that point.<ref>{{cite news | first = Gabriel | last = Snyder | url = https://variety.com/2005/digital/features/it-s-a-jungle-out-there-2-1117923293/ | title = It's a jungle out there | work = [[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=May 22, 2005| access-date=April 28, 2023| archive-date = August 26, 2022 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20220826010145/https://variety.com/2005/digital/features/it-s-a-jungle-out-there-2-1117923293/ | url-status=live}}</ref> Jackson initially turned down the offer but reneged on his decision after fearing that the remake would fail under a different director.{{sfn|Pryor|2004|pp=209–210}} Around that time, Jackson was attached to direct live-action film adaptations of ''[[The Hobbit]]'' and ''[[The Lord of the Rings]]'' but proceeded with ''King Kong'' due to producer [[Harvey Weinstein]] taking too long to acquire the rights. 

To placate Weinstein's anger, Jackson brokered a co-financing/co-distributing deal with [[Miramax]] for ''King Kong'' that benefited all parties. Pre-production commenced with filmmaker [[Robert Zemeckis]] on board as executive producer and Universal approved the script co-written by Jackson and his wife [[Fran Walsh]], with filming planned to commence in 1997 for a potential 1998 release.{{sfn|Morton|2005|pp=316–329}} However, Universal halted progress in January 1997 after growing concern over competition from the then-upcoming releases of [[Godzilla (1998 film)|TriStar's ''Godzilla'']] and [[Mighty Joe Young (1998 film)|''Mighty Joe Young'']], both released in 1998, the latter by Miramax's parent company, Disney.<ref name=going>{{cite magazine | first = Michael | last = Fleming | url = https://variety.com/2003/film/markets-festivals/universal-going-ape-for-kong-1117883851/ | title = Universal going ape for 'Kong' | magazine = [[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] | date =March 30, 2003| access-date =April 28, 2023| archive-date = August 5, 2022 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20220805042133/https://variety.com/2003/film/markets-festivals/universal-going-ape-for-kong-1117883851/ | url-status = live }}</ref> Universal aborted ''King Kong'' the following month, despite [[Wētā FX]] and [[Wētā Workshop]] having already designed six months worth of pre-production, and Jackson reverted to helm [[The Lord of the Rings (film series)|''The Lord of the Rings'' trilogy]].{{sfn|Woods|2005|pp=176–187}}{{sfn|Pryor|2004|pp=209–210}} Years later, Jackson, and his frequent collaborator [[Christian Rivers]], had noted a similar tone between Jackson's unused script and Universal's 1999 remake of ''[[The Mummy (1999 film)|The Mummy]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://collider.com/peter-jackson-king-kong-original-version/|title=Peter Jackson's Original Version of ''King Kong'' Was Very, Very Different|first=Adam|last=Chitwood|work=Collider |date=February 5, 2021|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428185515/https://collider.com/peter-jackson-king-kong-original-version/}}</ref>

In 1998, [[Warner Bros.]] released ''[[The Mighty Kong]]'' direct-to-video. An animated musical remake of the 1933 film, it features the voices of [[Dudley Moore]], [[Jodi Benson]], [[Bill Sage]], [[Jason Gray-Stanford]], and [[Richard Newman (actor)|Richard Newman]]. The film received negative reviews and is placed in the low-tier end of most ranking lists of ''King Kong'' films.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cbr.com/king-of-kong-every-king-kong-movie-ranked-from-worst-to-best/#the-mighty-kong-1998|title=King of Kong: Every "King Kong" Movie Ranked From Worst to Best|first=Nigel|last=Mitchell|work=CBR.com|date=August 5, 2016|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428191747/https://www.cbr.com/king-of-kong-every-king-kong-movie-ranked-from-worst-to-best/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://screencrush.com/every-king-kong-movie-ranked/|title=Every 'King Kong' Movie Ranked From Worst to Best|first=Claire|last=Epting|work=ScreenCrush|date=March 12, 2021|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428192003/https://screencrush.com/every-king-kong-movie-ranked/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.slashfilm.com/624934/every-king-kong-movie-ranked-from-worst-to-best/|title=Every King Kong Movie Ranked From Worst To Best|first=Dalin|last=Rowell|work=[[/Film]]|date=October 5, 2021|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428192126/https://www.slashfilm.com/624934/every-king-kong-movie-ranked-from-worst-to-best/}}</ref>

===2000s===
{{main|Kong: The Animated Series|King Kong (2005 film)}}
In response to the success of [[Columbia TriStar Television]]'s animated series ''[[Godzilla: The Series]]'', [[BKN International]] produced ''[[Kong: The Animated Series]]'' and ran on syndicated television between 2000 and 2001. The series is a direct sequel to the 1933 film and focuses on a new Kong cloned from the DNA of both the 1933 Kong and a young man named Jason and as a result, Jason is able to link with Kong via a special device in moments of crisis. The series generated two direct-to-video animated films, ''Kong: King of Atlantis'' (2005) and ''Kong: Return to the Jungle'' (2006).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.filmaffinity.com/au/film390644.html|title=Kong: The Animated Series (TV Series)|first=|last=|work=[[FilmAffinity]]|date=|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428194810/https://www.filmaffinity.com/au/film390644.html}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://screenrant.com/kong-animated-series-resurrection-movie-death-cloning/|title=How Kong: The Animated Series Resurrected The Ape Following His Movie Death|first=Padraig|last=Cotter|work=Screen Rant|date=January 9, 2022|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20230428190445/https://screenrant.com/kong-animated-series-resurrection-movie-death-cloning/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://movieweb.com/king-kong-movies-in-order/?newsletter_popup=1|title=King Kong: All Movies in Order|first=Alyssa|last=Gawaran|work=MovieWeb|date=May 17, 2022|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428195027/https://movieweb.com/king-kong-movies-in-order/?newsletter_popup=1}}</ref>

After the success of ''The Lord of the Rings'' films, ''[[The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring|The Fellowship of the Ring]]'' and ''[[The Lord of the Rings: The Two Towers|The Two Towers]]'', Universal approached Jackson in early 2003 during post-production on ''[[The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King|The Return of the King]]'' about reviving his ''King Kong'' remake. In March 2003, Universal scheduled a 2005 release date and [[Philippa Boyens]] was hired to rewrite Jackson's and Walsh's 1996 script, but Jackson began to work on a new script in October 2003 that was closer to the 1933 film after being dissatisfied with the 1996 script. They included unused elements from [[James Ashmore Creelman]]'s script of the 1933 film, such as the lost spider-pit scene. [[Delos W. Lovelace]]'s 1932 novelization was also a source of inspiration. Weta Workshop and Weta Digital began to work immediately on ''King Kong'' after the completion of ''The Return of the King'' and Jackson had brought back the crew that worked with him on ''The Lord of the Rings'' trilogy. Filming began in September 2004, with [[Andy Serkis]] portraying Kong via [[motion-capture acting]].<ref name=going/>{{sfn|Woods|2005|pp=176–187}}{{sfn|Morton|2005|pp=316–329}}{{sfn|Sibley|2006|pp=526–542}} Universal theatrically released ''[[King Kong (2005 film)|King Kong]]'' on December 13, 2005, to generally positive reviews, with the film appearing in several top ten lists for 2005.<ref>{{cite web | title=The 2005 Top Ten's | work=Awards Watch | url=http://www.moviecitynews.com/awards/2006/top_tens/critics_01.html|access-date=2006-05-11 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20060324050559/http://www.moviecitynews.com/awards/2006/top_tens/critics_01.html |archive-date = March 24, 2006}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| author=Ebert, Roger| date=December 18, 2005| title=Ebert's Best 10 Movies of 2005| work=rogerebert.com| url=http://rogerebert.suntimes.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=%2F20051218%2FCOMMENTARY%2F512180302| access-date=2006-05-11| archive-date=November 17, 2012| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121117004020/http://rogerebert.suntimes.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=%2F20051218%2FCOMMENTARY%2F512180302| url-status=dead}}</ref> The film received four [[Academy Award]] nominations, for [[Academy Award for Best Visual Effects|Visual Effects]], [[Academy Award for Best Sound|Sound Mixing]], [[Academy Award for Best Sound Editing|Sound Editing]], and [[Academy Award for Best Production Design|Production Design]], winning all but the last.<ref name="Oscars2006">{{Cite web |url=http://www.oscars.org/awards/academyawards/legacy/ceremony/78th-winners.html |title=The 78th Academy Awards (2006) Nominees and Winners |access-date=April 28, 2023|work=oscars.org |archive-date=September 29, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120929062922/http://www.oscars.org/awards/academyawards/legacy/ceremony/78th-winners.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news | title=Oscars 2006: The nominees | work=BBC News | url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/4665684.stm | access-date=April 28, 2023| date=January 31, 2006| location=London | archive-date=February 3, 2006 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060203023231/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/entertainment/4665684.stm | url-status=live }}</ref> Jackson had also directed a recreation of the lost spider pit sequence that was featured on the 2005 DVD release of the 1933 film.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dvdtalk.com/dvdsavant/s1821kong.html|title=DVD Savant Review: King Kong Two Disc Special Edition (1933)|first=Glenn|last=Erickson|work=DVDTalk|date=November 21, 2005|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428204830/https://www.dvdtalk.com/dvdsavant/s1821kong.html}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fangoria.com/original/beast-master-king-kong-at-90/|title=Beast Master: King Kong At 90|first=Rich|last=Johnson|work=Fangoria|date=April 7, 2023|access-date=April 28, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 28, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428204654/https://www.fangoria.com/original/beast-master-king-kong-at-90/}}</ref>

===2010s–present===
==== Monsterverse ====
{{main|Monsterverse|Kong (Monsterverse)}}
In 2013, Jackson and producer [[Mary Parent]] had intended to produce ''Skull Island'', a direct sequel to his 2005 remake and handpicked then up-and-coming director [[Adam Wingard]] to direct it. However, when [[Legendary Entertainment|Legendary Pictures]] took over the project and redeveloped it as a prequel, Jackson and Wingard dropped out but Parent remained; Parent eventually became Legendary's production chief.<ref name="Davids">{{cite web|last=Davids|first=Brian|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/why-godzilla-vs-kong-director-adam-wingard-treated-kong-like-an-80s-action-hero|title=Why ''Godzilla vs. Kong'' Director Adam Wingard Treated Kong Like an '80s Action Hero|date=March 30, 2021|website=The Hollywood Reporter|accessdate=March 31, 2021|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507173225/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/why-godzilla-vs-kong-director-adam-wingard-treated-kong-like-an-80s-action-hero-4158496/}}</ref>

In July 2014, Legendary announced the film at [[San Diego Comic-Con]]'s Hall H with [[Max Borenstein]] (writer of Legendary's 2014 ''[[Godzilla (2014 film)|Godzilla]]'' film) to write the script. Legendary initially targeted a November 4, 2016 release and had offered [[Joe Cornish]] to direct it.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2014/07/comic-con-legendary-wants-joe-cornish-for-skull-island-811426/|title=Comic-Con: Legendary Wants Joe Cornish for ''Skull Island''|first=Mike Jr.|last=Fleming|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=July 28, 2014|access-date=May 7, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507171830/https://deadline.com/2014/07/comic-con-legendary-wants-joe-cornish-for-skull-island-811426/}}</ref> Around that time, Jackson expressed interest in seeing [[Guillermo del Toro]] direct the film.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.slashfilm.com/peter-jackson-skull-island-guillermo-del-toro/ |title=Peter Jackson Would Like Guillermo Del Toro to Direct 'Skull Island' |work=[[/Film]] |date=July 31, 2014 |access-date=July 15, 2016 |last=Han |first=Angie |url-status=live |archive-date=May 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20230507170530/https://www.slashfilm.com/533110/peter-jackson-skull-island-guillermo-del-toro/ }}</ref> In September 2014, Legendary announced [[Jordan Vogt-Roberts]] as the director, with [[Tom Hiddleston]] leading the film.<ref name="HiddlestonCast">{{cite web |url=https://deadline.com/2014/09/skull-island-tom-hiddleston-king-kong-835067/ |title=Legendary's 'Skull Island'; Tom Hiddleston Stars, Jordan Vogt-Roberts Helms King Kong Origin Tale |last=Fleming |first=Mike Jr. |website=Deadline Hollywood |date=September 16, 2014 |access-date=September 16, 2014|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507174411/https://deadline.com/2014/09/skull-island-tom-hiddleston-king-kong-835067/}}</ref> In September 2015, Legendary moved the film, retitled ''[[Kong: Skull Island]]'', from Universal to Warner Bros. to unite the King Kong and Godzilla properties under one studio.<ref name="KingKongOnMove">{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2015/09/king-kong-godzilla-legendary-monster-movie-warner-bros-1201521004/|title=King Kong On Move To Warner Bros, Presaging Godzilla Monster Matchup|last=Fleming|first=Mike Jr.|work=Deadline|date=September 10, 2015|access-date=September 10, 2015|archive-date=September 11, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150911232832/http://deadline.com/2015/09/king-kong-godzilla-legendary-monster-movie-warner-bros-1201521004/|url-status=live}}</ref> In October 2015, Legendary officially announced their plans for a shared cinematic franchise between King Kong and Godzilla that would culminate in a ''[[Godzilla vs. Kong]]'' film, at the time slated for a 2020 release.<ref name="ann">{{cite press release|url=http://www.legendary.com/legendary-and-warner-bros-pictures-announce-cinematic-franchise-uniting-godzilla-king-kong-and-other-iconic-giant-monsters/|title=Legendary and Warner Bros. Pictures Announce Cinematic Franchise Uniting Godzilla, King Kong and Other Iconic Giant Monsters|publisher=Legendary Pictures|date=October 14, 2015|access-date=October 14, 2015|archive-date=November 5, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151105182819/http://www.legendary.com/legendary-and-warner-bros-pictures-announce-cinematic-franchise-uniting-godzilla-king-kong-and-other-iconic-giant-monsters/|url-status=live}}</ref> In January 2017, Legendary's Godzilla–Kong series was revealed as the [[Monsterverse]], after it was announced that Legendary founder [[Thomas Tull]] would resign but remain as producer on the Monsterverse films.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/thomas-tull-exit-legendary-entertainment-965108|title=Thomas Tull to Exit Legendary Entertainment (Exclusive)|last1=Ford|first1=Rebecca|last2=Masters|first2=Kim|work=The Hollywood Reporter|date=January 17, 2017|access-date=January 20, 2017|archive-date=January 19, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170119185527/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/thomas-tull-exit-legendary-entertainment-965108|url-status=live}}</ref>

Inspired by ''[[Heart of Darkness]]'' and ''[[Apocalypse Now]]'', Borenstein had originally envisioned a [[war film]] that began in the [[Vietnam War]] and jumped to the present day; however, Legendary rejected this idea. Borenstein then wrote a draft set in 1917 while retaining the ''Apocalypse Now'' concept. That version saw Hiddleston playing a character leading a rescue mission into Skull Island to find his brother, who came to the island in search of a cure-all serum.<ref name="McKittrick">{{cite web |last=McKittrick |first=Christopher |title='Every movie is its own beast.' Max Borenstein on Kong: Skull Island |url=https://creativescreenwriting.com/kong/ |access-date=March 10, 2017 |publisher=Creative Screenwriting |date=March 9, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Sampson |first=Mike |title=Has the 'Kong: Skull Island' Plot Been Revealed? |url=https://screencrush.com/kong-skull-island-plot-details-revealed/ |access-date=August 9, 2018 |website=ScreenCrush |date=September 15, 2015}}</ref> After Vogt-Roberts joined the film, he liked Borenstein's original Vietname-era pitch and convinced Legendary to set the film near the end of the Vietnam war.<ref>{{cite web |last=Eisenberg |first=Eric |title=One Change Kong: Skull Island Made To Its Script That Resulted in a Totally Different Movie |url=https://www.cinemablend.com/news/1633919/one-change-kong-skull-island-made-to-its-script-that-resulted-in-a-totally-different-movie |publisher=Cinemablend |access-date=August 9, 2018 |date=March 10, 2017}}</ref> For Kong's design, Vogt-Roberts took the character back the look of the 1933 iteration, wanting him to look straightforward yet iconic enough that a child could draw him, and wanted Kong to reflect a lonely and morose God.<ref name="design">{{cite magazine |url=https://www.ew.com/article/2016/11/10/kong-skull-island-first-look-movie-monster |title=Kong: Skull Island unleashes exclusive first look at the movie monster |last=Smith |first=C. Molly |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |date=November 10, 2016 |access-date=November 16, 2016}}</ref> [[Terry Notary]] portrayed Kong via performance capture, while [[Toby Kebbell]] provided additional facial work; Notary sought Serkis' blessing before accepting the role.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/article/2016/05/11/kong-skull-island-toby-kebbell/|title=Kong: Skull Island: Toby Kebbell reveals who is playing King Kong|first=Kevin P.|last=Sullivan|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|date=May 11, 2016|access-date=May 7, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20230507180527/https://ew.com/article/2016/05/11/kong-skull-island-toby-kebbell/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/kong-skull-island-kong-actor-sequel-plans-andy-serkis-blessing-982044/|title=''Skull Island'': Kong Motion-Capture Actor on Sequel Plans and Seeking Andy Serkis' Blessing?|first=Patrick|last=Shanley|work=The Hollywood Reporter|date=March 10, 2017|access-date=May 7, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507185944/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/kong-skull-island-kong-actor-sequel-plans-andy-serkis-blessing-982044/}}</ref> [[Principal photography]] began in October 2015, in [[Hawaii]] and [[Vietnam]] and wrapped in March 2016.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.instagram.com/p/BDFTAQLDI-r/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/iarchive/s/instagram/BDFTAQLDI-r |archive-date=December 26, 2021 |url-access=registration|title=That's a wrap. 📷 by @tutututuuuu |publisher=[[Instagram.com]] |first=Thomas |last=Mann |date=March 18, 2016 |access-date=March 20, 2016}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

''[[Kong: Skull Island]]'' was released on March 10, 2017, to positive reviews,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://collider.com/kong-skull-island-fan-reviews/|title='Kong: Skull Island': What Did You Think?|last=Goldberg|first=Matt|work=Collider|date=March 10, 2017|access-date=March 15, 2017|archive-date=March 13, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170313212412/http://collider.com/kong-skull-island-fan-reviews/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/scottmendelson/2017/03/13/kong-skull-island-four-lessons-for-justice-league-and-the-dc-films-franchise/#40b23ec84168|title='Kong: Skull Island': Four Lessons For 'Justice League' And The DC Films Franchise|last=Mendelson|first=Scott|work=Forbes|date=March 13, 2017|access-date=March 15, 2017|archive-date=March 13, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170313210239/https://www.forbes.com/sites/scottmendelson/2017/03/13/kong-skull-island-four-lessons-for-justice-league-and-the-dc-films-franchise/#40b23ec84168|url-status=live}}</ref> and was a box office success, grossing $566 million worldwide against a budget of $185 million.<ref name=BOM>{{cite Box Office Mojo|id=3731562|title=Kong: Skull Island (2017)|accessdate=May 7, 2023}}</ref> The film received a nomination for [[Academy Award for Best Visual Effects|Best Visual Effects]] at the [[90th Academy Awards]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/lists/2018-visual-effects-society-awards-nominations-list-full-1067888/item/outstanding-visual-effects-a-photoreal-feature-1067977|title=Visual Effects Society Awards: 'Apes,' 'Blade Runner 2049' Lead Feature Nominees|last=Giardina|first=Carolyn|date=January 16, 2018|work=The Hollywood Reporter|access-date=July 15, 2018|archive-date=July 15, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180715181512/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/lists/2018-visual-effects-society-awards-nominations-list-full-1067888/item/outstanding-visual-effects-a-photoreal-feature-1067977|url-status=live}}</ref>

In April 2016, writer and artist Joe DeVito sued Legendary and Warner Bros. for breach of implied contract, alleging that they had stolen ideas and concepts from pitch meetings he had with Legendary in April 2014. He claimed Legendary turned him down yet used his ideas as the basis for their film without credit or compensation and sought $3.5 million in general and punitive damages.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/legendary-warner-bros-sued-allegedly-888503/|title=Legendary, Warner Bros. Sued for Allegedly Stealing ''Kong: Skull Island'' Story|first=Ashley|last=Cullins|work=The Hollywood Reporter|date=April 28, 2016|access-date=May 7, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507192648/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/legendary-warner-bros-sued-allegedly-888503/}}</ref>

In March 2017, Legendary had assembled a writers room for ''Godzilla vs. Kong'', with [[Terry Rossio]] (who had co-written an unproduced script for [[Godzilla (1998 film)|TriStar's ''Godzilla'']])<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.scifijapan.com/articles/2015/05/10/godzilla-unmade-the-history-of-jan-de-bonts-unproduced-tristar-film-part-1/|title=Godzilla Unmade: The History of Jan De Bont's Unproduced TriStar Film – Part 1 of 4|last=Aiken|first=Keith|website=Scifi Japan|date=May 10, 2015|access-date=May 23, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170915071019/http://www.scifijapan.com/articles/2015/05/10/godzilla-unmade-the-history-of-jan-de-bonts-unproduced-tristar-film-part-1/|archive-date=September 15, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref> leading a team consisting of Patrick McKay, J. D. Payne, Lindsey Beer, Cat Vasko, [[T. S. Nowlin]], Jack Paglen, and [[J. Michael Straczynski]].<ref name="writers room">{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/kong-skull-island-sequel-plans-godzilla-kong-sets-writers-room-984992|title='Godzilla vs. Kong' Film Sets Writers Room (Exclusive)|last=Kit|first=Borys|work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|date=March 10, 2017|access-date=March 10, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170310230537/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/kong-skull-island-sequel-plans-godzilla-kong-sets-writers-room-984992|archive-date=March 10, 2017|url-status=live}}</ref> At that time, Wingard had a general meeting with Parent, who recalled that Jackson had approved of Wingard and was announced as the director of ''Godzilla vs. Kong'' in May 2017.<ref name="Adam">{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/godzilla-kong-finds-director-adam-wingard-1008773|title='Godzilla vs. Kong' Finds Its Director With Adam Wingard (Exclusive)|last=Kit|first=Borys|work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|date=May 30, 2017|access-date=May 30, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170531025423/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/godzilla-kong-finds-director-adam-wingard-1008773|archive-date=May 31, 2017|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Davids"/>

Filming for ''Godzilla vs. Kong'' began in November 2018 in Hawaii, Australia, and Hong Kong and wrapped in April 2019.<ref name="Press Release">{{cite web|url=https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20181112005664/en/Warner-Bros.-Pictures%E2%80%99-Legendary-Entertainment%E2%80%99s-Monsterverse-Shifts|title=Warner Bros. Pictures' and Legendary Entertainment's Monsterverse Shifts into Overdrive as Cameras Roll on the Next Big-Screen Adventure "Godzilla Vs. Kong"|work=[[Business Wire]]|access-date=January 22, 2021|date=November 12, 2018|archive-date=January 20, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190120054526/https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20181112005664/en/Warner-Bros.-Pictures%E2%80%99-Legendary-Entertainment%E2%80%99s-Monsterverse-Shifts|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://ew.com/movies/2019/04/20/godzilla-vs-kong/|title=Godzilla vs. Kong wraps shooting|first=Clark|last=Collis|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|date=April 20, 2019|access-date=April 20, 2019|url-status=live|archive-date=April 20, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190420223019/https://ew.com/movies/2019/04/20/godzilla-vs-kong/}}</ref> Allan Henry provided the [[motion-capture acting|performance capture]] for Kong, while animation supervisor Eric Petey provided Kong's full body performance and [[Facial motion capture|facial capture]] for the ocean battle sequence.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://illusion-almanac.com/2021/05/28/extract-animating-kong-godzilla-vs-kong-visual-effects-by-weta-digital/|title=Extract: "Animating Kong" — Godzilla vs Kong visual effects by Weta Digital|first=|last=|work=The Illusion Almanac|date=May 28, 2021|access-date=March 29, 2024|url-status=live|archive-date=March 29, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240329164346/https://illusion-almanac.com/2021/05/28/extract-animating-kong-godzilla-vs-kong-visual-effects-by-weta-digital/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=April 15, 2021|title=How Kong's ocean showdown with Godzilla was made|url=https://beforesandafters.com/2021/04/15/how-kongs-ocean-showdown-with-godzilla-was-made/|website=Befores & Afters|language=en-US|first=Ian|last=Failes|access-date=April 15, 2021|url-status=live|archive-date=April 15, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210415230652/https://beforesandafters.com/2021/04/15/how-kongs-ocean-showdown-with-godzilla-was-made/}}</ref> The film endured several delays, largely due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], but was finally released internationally on March 24, 2021 and on March 31, 2021 in the United States; the latter which saw a concurrent release in theaters and on [[HBO Max]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.slashfilm.com/godzilla-vs-kong-clip/|title='Godzilla vs. Kong' Clip: Whoever Wins, Boats Lose|first=Ben|last=Pearson|work=[[/Film]]|date=February 26, 2021|access-date=March 2, 2021|url-status=live|archive-date=March 3, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210303031752/https://www.slashfilm.com/godzilla-vs-kong-clip/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Rubin|first=Rebecca|date=January 26, 2021|title='Godzilla vs. Kong' Release Date Moves Back a Week|url=https://variety.com/2021/film/news/godzilla-vs-kong-release-date-moves-1234889930/|access-date=January 26, 2021|website=Variety|url-status=live|archive-date=February 20, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210220164403/https://variety.com/2021/film/news/godzilla-vs-kong-release-date-moves-1234889930/}}</ref> The film received generally positive reviews and became a box office and streaming hit during the pandemic; it became the most successful launch item in HBO Max's history until it was overtaken by ''[[Mortal Kombat (2021 film)|Mortal Kombat]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.digitalspy.com/movies/a35970929/godzilla-vs-kong-rotten-tomatoes-score-reviews/|title=Godzilla vs Kong's Rotten Tomatoes score revealed as film gets positive reviews|first=Susannah|last=Alexander|work=[[Digital Spy]]|date=March 29, 2021|access-date=April 2, 2021|url-status=live|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210402212759/https://www.digitalspy.com/movies/a35970929/godzilla-vs-kong-rotten-tomatoes-score-reviews/}}</ref><ref name="breakeven">{{cite web |url= https://variety.com/2021/film/box-office/godzilla-vs-kong-opening-weekend-analysis-1234943958/ |title= 'Godzilla vs. Kong' Is a Box Office and HBO Max Hit. Will It Shake Up the Future of Moviegoing? |first=Rebecca|last=Rubin |work= [[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date= April 5, 2021 |access-date= April 5, 2021|url-status=live|archive-date=August 5, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210805101036/https://variety.com/2021/film/box-office/godzilla-vs-kong-opening-weekend-analysis-1234943958/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/vip/did-mortal-kombat-do-more-for-hbo-max-than-godzilla-vs-kong-1234964304/|title=Did 'Mortal Kombat' Do More for HBO Max Than 'Godzilla vs. Kong'?|first=Kevin|last=Tran|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=May 3, 2021|access-date=July 6, 2021|url-status=live|archive-date=May 5, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210505210821/https://variety.com/vip/did-mortal-kombat-do-more-for-hbo-max-than-godzilla-vs-kong-1234964304/}}</ref> The film grossed $470 million worldwide against a break-even point of $330 million,<ref name="breakeven"/><ref>{{cite Box Office Mojo|id= 5034838 |title= Godzilla vs. Kong|access-date= July 20, 2021}}</ref> and became the most pirated film of 2021.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thewrap.com/most-pirated-movies-tv-shows-2021-list/|title='Zack Snyder's Justice League,' 'Black Widow' Among 2021's Most Pirated Movies|first=Andi|last=Ortiz|work=TheWrap |date=January 26, 2022|access-date=February 2, 2022|url-status=live|archive-date= February 2, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220202174635/https://www.thewrap.com/most-pirated-movies-tv-shows-2021-list/}}</ref>

In January 2021, Legendary announced an animated series titled ''[[Skull Island (TV series)|Skull Island]]''. A joint-venture between [[Legendary Entertainment#Television|Legendary Television]], Tractor Pants Productions, [[Powerhouse Animation Studios]], and [[Netflix Animation]], the series was released on June 22, 2023, on Netflix.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2021/01/netflix-legendary-television-skull-island-tomb-raider-anime-series-1234681149/|title=Netflix And Legendary To Expand 'Skull Island' And 'Tomb Raider' Universes With New Anime Series|first=Dino|last=Ray-Ramos|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=January 27, 2021|access-date=January 27, 2021|url-status=live|archive-date=January 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210127214559/https://deadline.com/2021/01/netflix-legendary-television-skull-island-tomb-raider-anime-series-1234681149/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.joblo.com/skull-island-teaser-trailer/|title=Skull Island: teaser trailer released for Netflix's MonsterVerse animated series|first=Cody|last=Hamman|work=[[JoBlo.com]]|date=May 23, 2023|access-date=May 23, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 23, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230523180356/https://www.joblo.com/skull-island-teaser-trailer/}}</ref> In March 2025, Legendary Comics announced a partnership with Titan Comics to expand  ''Skull Island'' into a comic series titled ''Return to Skull Island'', slated for June 4, 2025.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/2025/tv/global/monsterverse-expands-return-to-skull-island-comic-books-1236331041/|title=MonsterVerse Expands With 'Return to Skull Island' Comic Books From Titan and Legendary (EXCLUSIVE)|first=Alex|last=Ritman|work=Variety|date=March 7, 2025|access-date=April 19, 2025|url-status=live|archive-date=April 19, 2025|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20250419160402/https://variety.com/2025/tv/global/monsterverse-expands-return-to-skull-island-comic-books-1236331041/}}</ref>

Due to the success of ''Godzilla vs. Kong'', Legendary announced a sequel in March 2022, scheduled to commence filming later that same year.<ref name="Variety March 2022">{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/2022/film/asia/godzilla-vs-kong-sequel-australia-1235210265/|title='Godzilla vs. Kong' Sequel to Film in Australia|first=Katherine|last=Tulich|work=Variety|date=March 20, 2022|access-date=March 20, 2022|url-status=live|archive-date=March 21, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220321052821/https://variety.com/2022/film/asia/godzilla-vs-kong-sequel-australia-1235210265/}}</ref> In May 2022, it was announced that Wingard would return to direct and that [[Dan Stevens]] had been cast in the lead. Wingard and Stevens had previously worked together on ''[[The Guest (2014 American film)|The Guest]]''.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Kroll |first1=Justin |title=''Godzilla vs. Kong 2'': Dan Stevens Reunites With ''The Guest'' Director Adam Wingard On Legendary Sequel |url=https://deadline.com/2022/05/godzilla-vs-kong-2-dan-stevens-the-guest-director-adam-wingard-legendary-1235022755/ |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |publisher=[[Penske Media Corporation]] |date=May 12, 2022|access-date=May 12, 2022|url-status=live|archive-date=May 12, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220512230601/https://deadline.com/2022/05/godzilla-vs-kong-2-dan-stevens-the-guest-director-adam-wingard-legendary-1235022755/}}</ref> Filming began in July 2022 in [[Gold Coast, Queensland]], and finished in November 2022.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.comingsoon.net/movies/news/1232875-legendary-godzilla-vs-kong-sequel-begins-production|title=Legendary's Godzilla vs. Kong Sequel Begins Production in Australia|first=Maggie Dela|last=Paz|work=Comingsoon.net|date=July 29, 2022|access-date=July 29, 2022|url-status=live|archive-date=July 29, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220729231454/https://www.comingsoon.net/movies/news/1232875-legendary-godzilla-vs-kong-sequel-begins-production}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://gizmodo.com/godzilla-vs-kong-2-title-confirmed-1849749915|title=The Next Godzilla Movie Has an Unsurprising Title|first1=James|last2=Whitbrook|first2=Gordon|last1=Jackson|work=[[Gizmodo]]|date=November 11, 2022|access-date=November 12, 2022|url-status=live|archive-date=November 12, 2022|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20221112231129/https://gizmodo.com/godzilla-vs-kong-2-title-confirmed-1849749915}}</ref> Allan Henry provided the performance capture for both the Skar King and Kong, reprising his role from ''Godzilla vs. Kong''.<ref name="GxK Mocap">{{cite web|url=https://collider.com/godzilla-x-kong-the-new-empire-fight-sequences-weta-vfx/|title=The Best Fight Sequence in 'Godzilla x Kong: The New Empire' Almost Didn't Make the Cut|first=Samantha|last=Coley|work=Collider|date=May 5, 2024|access-date=June 7, 2024|url-status=live|archive-date=June 8, 2024|archive-url=https://archive.today/20240608030751/https://collider.com/godzilla-x-kong-the-new-empire-fight-sequences-weta-vfx/}}</ref> In April 2023, Legendary released a teaser trailer revealing the film's official title as ''[[Godzilla x Kong: The New Empire]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/2023/film/news/film-news-in-brief-april-17-1235585251/|title=''Godzilla x Kong'' Sequel Gets New Title – Film News in Brief|first1=Jazz|last1=Tangcay|first2=Charna|last2=Flam|work=Variety|date=April 19, 2023|access-date=April 19, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=April 20, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230420031026/https://variety.com/2023/film/news/film-news-in-brief-april-17-1235585251/}}</ref> The film was theatrically released on March 29, 2024.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/godzilla-x-kong-moves-up-release-theaters-mickey-17-delayed-1235783720/|title='Godzilla x Kong' Moves Up Release in Theaters to Late March, 'Mickey 17' Delayed|first=Pamela|last=McClintock|work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|date=January 9, 2024|access-date=January 9, 2024|url-status=live|archive-date=January 10, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240110000900/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/godzilla-x-kong-moves-up-release-theaters-mickey-17-delayed-1235783720/}}</ref> Due to the film's success, Legendary announced in May 2024 that a follow-up is in development and would be written by [[David Callaham]].<ref name="May 2024"/>

In January 2024, Kong briefly appeared in the finale of the television series ''[[Monarch: Legacy of Monsters]]''.<ref name="Monarch Cameo">{{cite web|url=https://screenrant.com/monarch-season-1-finale-kong-cameo-creator-explained/|title=Monarch Season 1 Ending & Surprise Cameo Explained By Creators|first=Ryan|last=Northrup|work=[[Screenrant]]|date=January 12, 2024|access-date=January 30, 2024|url-status=live|archive-date=February 28, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260228175455/https://screenrant.com/monarch-season-1-finale-kong-cameo-creator-explained/}}</ref> The character's role would further be expanded on in the series' second season.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://bloody-disgusting.com/editorials/3937154/king-kong-television-history/|title=Titan of Television: A History of King Kong on the Small Screen|first=Luiz|last=H.C.|work=Bloody Disgusting|date=February 26, 2026|access-date=February 28, 2026|url-status=live|archive-date=February 28, 2026|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/2z7Oh}}</ref>

Due to the success of ''Godzilla x Kong: The New Empire'', Legendary announced in May 2024 that a sequel, titled ''[[Godzilla x Kong: Supernova]]'', had entered development and would be written by [[David Callaham]].<ref name="May 2024">{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/godzilla-x-kong-follow-dave-callaham-1235895879/|title='Godzilla x Kong' Follow-Up Enlists 'Shang Chi' Scribe Dave Callaham to Write (Exclusive)|first=Borys|last=Kit|website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=May 10, 2024|access-date=May 10, 2024|url-status=live|archive-date=May 10, 2024|archive-url=https://archive.today/20240510201312/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/godzilla-x-kong-follow-dave-callaham-1235895879/}}</ref> The following week, Legendary announced that Wingard would not return to direct due to scheduling conflicts, but had expressed interest in having Wingard return.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/adam-wingard-not-returning-godzilla-x-kong-sequel-exclusive-1235905822/|title=Adam Wingard Not Returning for 'Godzilla x Kong' Sequel (Exclusive)|first=Borys|last=Kit|work=The Hollywood Reporter|date=May 21, 2024|access-date=May 21, 2024|url-status=live|archive-date=May 22, 2024|archive-url=https://archive.today/20240522003737/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/adam-wingard-not-returning-godzilla-x-kong-sequel-exclusive-1235905822/}}</ref> In June 2024, Legendary announced [[Grant Sputore]] (director of ''[[I Am Mother]]'') as the new director and that the film would be released on March 26, 2027.<ref name="Grant and release">{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/godzilla-x-kong-sequel-director-1235916872/|title=Legendary's 'Godzilla x Kong' Followup Sets Director Grant Sputore|first=Aaron|last=Couch|work=The Hollywood Reporter|date=June 6, 2024|access-date=June 6, 2024|url-status=live|archive-date=June 6, 2024|archive-url=https://archive.today/20240606235517/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/godzilla-x-kong-sequel-director-1235916872/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/2024/film/news/warner-bros-legendary-denis-villeneuve-1236056852/|title=Warner Bros. and Legendary Set Denis Villeneuve Event Film for 2026, Next MonsterVerse Movie for 2027|first=Katcy|last=Stephan|work=Variety|date=June 28, 2024|access-date=June 28, 2024|url-status=live|archive-date=June 28, 2024|archive-url=https://archive.today/20240628224858/https://variety.com/2024/film/news/warner-bros-legendary-denis-villeneuve-1236056852/}}</ref> Pre-production began in January 2025, with performers being cast such as [[Kaitlyn Dever]], [[Jack O'Connell (actor)|Jack O'Connell]], [[Delroy Lindo]], Dan Stevens (reprises his role from the previous film), [[Matthew Modine]], [[Alycia Debnam-Carey]], and [[Sam Neill]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2025/01/godzilla-x-kong-sequel-kaitlyn-dever-1236265739/|title=Legendary's 'Godzilla x Kong' Sequel Adds Kaitlyn Dever To Cast|first=Justin|last=Kroll|work=Deadline|date=January 24, 2025|access-date=January 24, 2025|url-status=live|archive-date=January 25, 2025|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20250125050141/https://deadline.com/2025/01/godzilla-x-kong-sequel-kaitlyn-dever-1236265739/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Grobar |first=Matt |date=April 15, 2025|title=Sam Neill Boards Legendary's Next Monsterverse Film |url=https://deadline.com/2025/04/godzilla-x-kong-sequel-adds-sam-neill-1236369130/ |access-date=2025-04-15 |website=Deadline |language=en-US|url-status=live|archive-date=April 16, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250416230106/https://deadline.com/2025/04/godzilla-x-kong-sequel-adds-sam-neill-1236369130/}}</ref>

====Other projects ====
{{main|Kong: King of the Apes}}
In 2016, [[Netflix]] released the animated series ''[[Kong: King of the Apes]]''; a joint venture between [[41 Entertainment]] and [[Avi Arad]], the series ran for two seasons.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/2014/digital/news/netflix-orders-king-kong-movie-animated-series-from-avi-arad-1201318424/|title= Netflix Orders King Kong Movie, Animated Series from Avi Arad|first=Todd|last=Spangler|work=Variety|date=October 1, 2014|access-date=May 7, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507194623/https://variety.com/2014/digital/news/netflix-orders-king-kong-movie-animated-series-from-avi-arad-1201318424/}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cnet.com/tech/services-and-software/netpicks-everything-coming-and-going-on-netflix-for-april-2016/|title=Everything coming and going on Netflix for April 2016|first=Iyaz|last=Akhtar|work=[[CNET]]|date=March 22, 2016|access-date=May 7, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20230507190510/https://www.cnet.com/tech/services-and-software/netpicks-everything-coming-and-going-on-netflix-for-april-2016/}}</ref>

In April 2017, [[MarVista Entertainment]] and [[IM Global]] announced their own live-action King Kong project for television; written by Jonathan Penner and Stacy Title, the series is to be based on DeVito's materials, approved by the Cooper estate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2017/04/king-kong-tv-series-female-lead-marvista-entertainment-im-global-tv-1202071347/|title=King Kong Female-Led TV Series In Works At MarVista Entertainment & IM Global TV|first=Nellie|last=Andreeva|work=Deadline Hollywood|date=April 18, 2017|access-date=May 7, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507195903/https://deadline.com/2017/04/king-kong-tv-series-female-lead-marvista-entertainment-im-global-tv-1202071347/}}</ref>

In August 2022, [[Disney Branded Television]] announced plans for a live-action series of their own for [[Disney+]]. The series is to be co-produced by [[James Wan]]'s Atomic Monster Productions and written by [[Stephany Folsom]], and would be based on Cooper's original books and DeVito's new novelizations, focusing on a new Kong in modern times with characters exploring the origins and mysteries of his home island.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2022/08/king-kong-series-disney-plus-stephany-folsom-james-wan-atomic-monster-disney-branded-television-1235098603/|title=''King Kong'' Live-Action Series in Works at Disney+ from Stephany Folsom, James Wan's Atomic Monster & Disney Branded TV|first=Nellie|last=Andreeva|work=Deadline Hollywood|date=August 23, 2022|access-date=May 7, 2023|url-status=live|archive-date=May 7, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507203533/https://deadline.com/2022/08/king-kong-series-disney-plus-stephany-folsom-james-wan-atomic-monster-disney-branded-television-1235098603/}}</ref>

