==Cultural impact==
{{Main|King Kong in popular culture}}
King Kong, as well as the series of films featuring him, have been featured many times in popular culture outside of the films themselves, in forms ranging from straight copies to parodies and joke references, and in media from comic books to video games. [[The Beatles]]' 1968 animated film ''[[Yellow Submarine (1968 film)|Yellow Submarine]]'' includes a scene of the characters opening a door to reveal King Kong abducting a woman from her bed. ''[[The Simpsons]]'' episode "[[Treehouse of Horror III]]" features a segment called "King Homer" which parodies the plot of the original film, with [[Homer Simpson|Homer]] as Kong and [[Marge Simpson|Marge]] in the [[Ann Darrow]] role. It ends with King Homer marrying Marge and eating her father.{{citation needed|date=October 2018}}

The British comedy TV series ''[[The Goodies (TV series)|The Goodies]]'' made an episode called "[[Kitten Kong]]", in which a giant cat called Twinkle roams the streets of [[London]], knocking over the [[British Telecom Tower]]. The controversial [[World War II]] [[Dutch resistance]] fighter [[Christiaan Lindemans]] — eventually arrested on suspicion of having betrayed secrets to the Nazis — was nicknamed "King Kong" due to his being exceptionally tall.<ref>{{cite book |title=British Intelligence in the Second World War: Volume 4, Security and Counter-Intelligence |year=1990 |page=373 |first1=F. H. |last1=Hinsley |first2=C. A. G. |last2=Simkins |publisher=Cambridge University Press}}</ref> [[Frank Zappa]] and [[The Mothers of Invention]] recorded an instrumental about "King Kong" in 1967 and featured it on the album ''[[Uncle Meat]]''. Zappa went on to make many other versions of the song on albums such as ''[[Make a Jazz Noise Here]]'', ''[[You Can't Do That on Stage Anymore, Vol. 3]]'', ''[[Ahead of Their Time]]'', and ''[[Beat the Boots]]''.

[[The Kinks]] recorded a song called "King Kong" as the B-side to their 1969 "[[Plastic Man (song)|Plastic Man]]" single. In 1972, a {{convert|550|cm|ft|0|abbr=on}} fiberglass [[King Kong statue|statue of King Kong]] was erected in Birmingham, England. The second track of [[The Jimmy Castor Bunch]] album ''Supersound'' from 1975 is titled "King Kong".<ref>{{cite web|title=AllMusic|website=[[AllMusic]]|url=https://www.allmusic.com/album/super-sound-mw0000859947|access-date=August 20, 2013|archive-date=June 28, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150628215020/http://www.allmusic.com/album/super-sound-mw0000859947|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Filk Music]] artists [[Ookla the Mok (band)|Ookla the Mok]]'s "Song of Kong", which explores the reasons why King Kong and Godzilla should not be roommates, appears on their 2001 album ''Smell No Evil''.{{citation needed|date=October 2018}} [[Daniel Johnston]] wrote and recorded a song called "King Kong" on his fifth self-released [[music cassette]], ''[[Yip/Jump Music]]'' in 1983, rereleased on [[CD]] and double [[LP album|LP]] by [[Homestead Records]] in 1988. The song is an [[a cappella]] narrative of the original movie's story line. [[Tom Waits]] recorded a cover version of the song with various sound effects on the 2004 release, ''[[The Late Great Daniel Johnston: Discovered Covered]]''. [[ABBA]] recorded "[[King Kong Song]]" for their 1974 album ''[[Waterloo (album)|Waterloo]]'' and it was included as a B-side on the singles for "[[Honey, Honey]]" and "[[I've Been Waiting for You (ABBA song)|I've Been Waiting for You]]".

