Various electronic games featuring King Kong have been released through the years by numerous companies. These range from handheld LCD games, to video games, to pinball machines. 

[[Tiger Electronics]] released various King Kong games in the early 1980s, including a Tabletop LCD game in 1981,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKong.htm |title=Tiger King Kong |publisher=Handheldmuseum.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=March 1, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090301000309/http://handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKong.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> [[King Kong (1982 video game)|a video game for the Atari 2600]] home video game system in 1982,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.gamefaqs.com/console/a2600/image/563246.html?box=28936 |title=King Kong (US) Box Shot for Atari 2600 |publisher=GameFAQs |date=June 2, 2009 |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=July 3, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090703182739/http://www.gamefaqs.com/console/a2600/image/563246.html?box=28936 |url-status=live }}</ref> a handheld game in 1982 in both a regular edition<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKongLCD.htm |title=Tiger King Kong LCD |publisher=Handheldmuseum.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=July 20, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090720101230/http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKongLCD.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> a large screen edition<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKongLS.htm |title=Tiger King Kong Large Screen |publisher=Handheldmuseum.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=March 27, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100327022935/http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKongLS.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> (the regular edition was later reissued by [[Tandy Computers|Tandy]] in 1984<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tandy/KingKong.htm|title=Tandy King Kong LCD|publisher=Handheldmuseum.com|access-date=February 11, 2011|archive-date=June 24, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110624022136/http://handheldmuseum.com/Tandy/KingKong.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>), an "Orlitronic" game (for the international markets) in 1983,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKongOrlitronic.htm |title=Tiger King Kong (Orlitronic) |publisher=Handheldmuseum.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=February 4, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100204212822/http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKongOrlitronic.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> and a color "Flip-Up" game in 1984.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKongColor.htm |title=Tiger King Kong Color |publisher=Handheldmuseum.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=February 17, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100217073708/http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Tiger/KingKongColor.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Epoch Co.]] released two LCD games in 1982. One was ''King Kong: New York'',<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Grandstand/KingKong.htm |title=Grandstand King Kong New York |publisher=Handheldmuseum.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=March 30, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100330091419/http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Grandstand/KingKong.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> and the other was ''King Kong: Jungle''<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.handheldmuseum.com/Epoch/KKJungle.htm |title=Epoch King Kong Jungle |publisher=Handheldmuseum.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=January 5, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100105174653/http://handheldmuseum.com/Epoch/KKJungle.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Konami]] released two games based on the film ''[[King Kong Lives]]'' in 1986. The first game was ''[[King Kong 2: Ikari no Megaton Punch]]'' for the Famicom, and the second was ''[[King Kong 2: Yomigaeru Densetsu]]'',<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.gamefaqs.com/computer/msx/image/918126.html |title=King Kong 2 Box Shots and Screenshots for MSX |publisher=GameFAQs |date=June 2, 2009 |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=July 3, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090703172703/http://www.gamefaqs.com/computer/msx/image/918126.html |url-status=live }}</ref> for the MSX computer. In 1988, Konami featured the character in the crossover game ''[[Konami Wai Wai World]]''. All of these games were only released in Japan. 

In 1990 [[Data East]] planned to release a pinball machine called ''King Kong: The Eighth Wonder of the World''<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ipdb.org/machine.cgi?id=3194 |title=The Internet Pinball Machine Database |publisher=Ipdb.org |date=November 20, 1931 |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=July 26, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110726185455/http://www.ipdb.org/machine.cgi?id=3194 |url-status=live }}</ref> in 1990, but only 9 units were made. In 1992, [[Nintendo]] produced an educational game called ''[[Mario is Missing]]'' that features a treasure hunt level involving King Kong in [[New York City]]. The character is represented by images of his arm grabbing the [[Empire State Building]] in the [[NES]] version and a full body statue in the [[SNES]] version. 

[[Bam! Entertainment]] released a Game Boy Advance game based on ''Kong: The Animated Series'' in 2002.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.gamefaqs.com/portable/gbadvance/image/567519.html?box=7985 |title=Kong: The Animated Series (US) Box Shot for Game Boy Advance |publisher=GameFAQs |date=June 2, 2009 |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=March 17, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230317173037/https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/gba/567519-kong-the-animated-series/media |url-status=live }}</ref> [[MGA Entertainment]] released an electronic handheld King Kong game (packaged with a small figurine) in 2003.<ref>[http://i54.tinypic.com/294sxtx.jpg Game photo] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160111204116/http://i54.tinypic.com/294sxtx.jpg |date=January 11, 2016 }} (jpg image). tinypic.com</ref> [[Majesco]] released a Game Boy Advance game based on the straight to video animated film ''Kong: King of Atlantis'' in 2005.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.gamefaqs.com/portable/gbadvance/image/930471.html?box=70998 |title=Kong: King of Atlantis (US) Box Shot for Game Boy Advance |publisher=GameFAQs |date=June 2, 2009 |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=July 3, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090703180308/http://www.gamefaqs.com/portable/gbadvance/image/930471.html?box=70998 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2005, [[Ubisoft]] released two video games based on the 2005 ''King Kong''. ''[[Peter Jackson's King Kong: The Official Game of the Movie]]'' was released on all video game platforms, while ''Kong: The 8th Wonder of the World'' was released for the Game Boy Advance. Also to tie into the film, [[Gameloft]] released ''King Kong: The Official Mobile Game of the Movie''<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/king-kong-the-official-game-of-the-movie-review/1900-6141335/|title=King Kong: The Official Game of the Movie Review|publisher=gamespot.com|date=December 15, 2005|access-date=June 9, 2019|archive-date=November 7, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181107185302/https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/king-kong-the-official-game-of-the-movie-review/1900-6141335/|url-status=live}}</ref> for mobile phones, while [[Radio Shack]] released a miniature pinball game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.worthpoint.com/worthopedia/radio-shack-king-kong-electronic-412840672|title=Radio Shack KING KONG Electronic Pinball Toy Game|publisher=worthpoint.com|access-date=June 9, 2019|archive-date=November 7, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181107145315/https://www.worthpoint.com/worthopedia/radio-shack-king-kong-electronic-412840672|url-status=live}}</ref> Taiyo Elec Co released a ''King Kong'' [[Pachinko]] game in 2007.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.taiyoelec.co.jp/user/pachinko/yearbook07.html |title=CR伝説の巫女 |access-date=March 5, 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100603041353/http://www.taiyoelec.co.jp/user/pachinko/yearbook07.html |archive-date=June 3, 2010 |language=ja }}. taiyoelec.co.jp (November 2007)</ref>

King Kong appears in the [[Warner Bros. Interactive Entertainment]] game ''[[Lego Dimensions]]''. He appears as a boss in ''[[Lego Dimensions#Story packs|The Lego Batman Movie]]'' pack.{{citation needed|date=November 2023}}

In 2021, Raw Thrills released a cinematic virtual reality motion game called ''King Kong of Skull Island''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.replaymag.com/raw-thrills-february-2021/#prettyPhoto|title=Raw Thrills Goes VR: King Kong of Skull Island VR First Venture Into Virtual for Legendary Game Maker|date=February 2021 |publisher=RePlaymag.com|access-date=February 1, 2021|archive-date=February 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210227122310/https://www.replaymag.com/raw-thrills-february-2021/#prettyPhoto|url-status=live}}</ref>

