In April 2025, [[Stern Pinball]] released a King Kong themed Pinball table called '''King Kong: Myth of Terror Island'''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wired.com/story/stern-pinball-king-kong-myth-of-terror-island/|title= I Played King Kong: Myth of Terror Island, Stern's Newest Pinball Game|access-date=April 16, 2025}}</ref>  The table comes in Pro, Premium, and Limited Editions.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sternpinball.com/game/king-kong/|title=New Release King Kong: Myth of Terror Island|access-date=April 16, 2025}}</ref>

In October 2025, [[IGG Inc.]] released a collaboration event with ''[[Godzilla x Kong: The New Empire]]'' and their RPG ''Doomsday: Last Survivors''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.scifijapan.com/news/doomsday-last-survivor-rpg-launches-godzilla-x-kong-the-new-empire-crossover|title=Doomsday: Last Survivors RPG Launches Godzilla x Kong: The New Empire Crossover|access-date=August 7, 2025}}</ref>

In April 2026, [[Call of Duty: Mobile]] featured an update for season four featuring a tie in with ''[[Godzilla x Kong: The New Empire]]''. It allows the players to use either King Kong or Godzilla as an operator.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://bloody-disgusting.com/video-games/3946933/call-of-duty-mobile-gets-godzilla-x-kong-crossover-for-season-4-update-trailer/|title='Call of Duty: Mobile' Gets 'Godzilla x Kong' Crossover for Season 4 Update|access-date=April 27, 2026}}</ref>

Besides starring in his own games, King Kong was the obvious influence behind other city-destroying gigantic apes, such as George from the ''[[Rampage (series)|Rampage]]'' series,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/the-creator-of-rampage-reveals-the-games-undiscovered-easter-egg-and-gushes-about-the-rock |title=The Creator of Rampage Reveals the Game's Undiscovered Easter Egg and Gushes about The Rock |publisher=syfywire |access-date=April 12, 2018 |archive-date=April 11, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180411162211/http://www.syfy.com/syfywire/the-creator-of-rampage-reveals-the-games-undiscovered-easter-egg-and-gushes-about-the-rock |url-status=live }}</ref> Woo from ''[[King of the Monsters (video game)|King of the Monsters]]'' (who was modeled after the Toho version of the character), and Congar from ''[[War of the Monsters]]'', as well as giant apes worshipped as deities, like Chaos and Blizzard from ''[[Primal Rage]]'' whose beast classification is listed as "Kong" on the toy packaging.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=News Bits |magazine=[[GamePro]] |issue=92 |publisher=[[International Data Group|IDG]]|date=May 1996|page=21}}</ref>

===Literature and comics===
{{Main|King Kong (comics)}}
[[File:Kingkong1932.jpg|thumb|Cover of the 1932 novelization of ''[[King Kong (1933 film)|King Kong]]'' written by [[Delos W. Lovelace]]. This novelization was released just over two months before the film premiered in New York City on March 2, 1933.]]
Over the decades, there have been numerous books, novels, and comic books based on King Kong by various publishers.

In December 1932, as the film ''[[King Kong (1933 film)|King Kong]]'' was finishing production, [[Merian C. Cooper]] asked his friend [[Delos W. Lovelace]] to adapt the film's screenplay into a novelization. Published by [[Grosset & Dunlap]], the book was released later that month on December 27,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.fullyarticulated.com/page30/page36/page35/page24/ |title=KING KONG Published Fiction, Pre-2005. The Original Hardcover |website=www.fullyarticulated.com |access-date=June 19, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170509054606/http://www.fullyarticulated.com/page30/page36/page35/page24/ |archive-date=May 9, 2017 |url-status=dead}}</ref> just over 2 months before the film premiered on March 2, 1933. This was a part of the film's advance marketing campaign. The novelization was credited as being based on the "Screenplay by James A. Creelman and Ruth Rose. Novelized from the Radio Picture". The byline written under the title was "Conceived by Edgar Wallace and Merian C. Cooper". However, despite the credit, Wallace had very little to do with the story or the character. In an interview, author-artist Joe DeVito explains:

{{blockquote|text=From what I know, Edgar Wallace, a famous writer of the time, died very early in the process. Little if anything of his ever appeared in the final story, but his name was retained for its saleability ... King Kong was Cooper's creation, a [[fantasy]] manifestation of his real life adventures. As many have mentioned before, Cooper was [[Carl Denham]]. His actual exploits rival anything [[Indiana Jones (character)|Indiana Jones]] ever did in the movies.|source=<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.scifidimensions.com/Dec04/joedevito.htm |title=Joe DeVito (Creator/Illustrator of Kong: King of Skull Island) |website=Scifidimensions.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110302124248/http://www.scifidimensions.com/Dec04/joedevito.htm |archive-date=March 2, 2011}}</ref>}}

This conclusion about Wallace's contribution was verified in the book ''The Making of King Kong'' by Orville Goldner and George E. Turner (1975) where Wallace stated in his diary, "Merian Cooper called and we talked over the big animal play we are going to write, or rather I am writing and he is directing", and "An announcement has been made in the local press that I am doing a super horror story with Merian Cooper, but the truth is it is much more his story than mine...I shall get much more credit out of the picture than I deserve if it is a success, but I shall be blamed by the public if it is a failure which seems fair".<ref>{{cite book|last1=Goldner|first1=Orville|last2=Turner|first2=George|title=The Making of King Kong: The Story Behind a Film Classic Hardcover |publisher=A.S. Barnes & Co|year=1975|isbn=978-0498015106|page=58}}</ref> Wallace died of [[pneumonia]] complicated by [[diabetes]] on February 10, 1932, and Cooper later had James Creelman and finally Ruth Rose finish the screenplay. Cooper would later state: "Actually, Edgar Wallace didn't write any of ''Kong'', not one bloody word...I'd promised him credit and so I gave it to him".<ref>{{cite book|last1=Goldner|first1=Orville|last2=Turner|first2=George|title=The Making of King Kong: The Story Behind a Film Classic Hardcover |publisher=A.S. Barnes & Co|year=1975|isbn=978-0498015106|page=59}}</ref>

Cooper issued a reprint of the novelization in 1965 that was published by [[Bantam Books]]. Some time later, the copyright expired and the publishing rights to the book fell into the public domain. Since then a myriad of publishers have reprinted the novelization numerous times.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://king-kong.fansforum.info/t283-KING-KONG-1932.htm|title=King Kong :: KING KONG (1932)|website=king-kong.fansforum.info|access-date=January 25, 2021|archive-date=January 31, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210131024327/https://king-kong.fansforum.info/t283-KING-KONG-1932.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> In 1983, Judith Conaway wrote a juvenile adaptation of the novelization called ''King Kong (Step Up Adventures)'' that featured illustrations by [[Mike Berenstain]] and was published by Random House books, while [[Anthony Browne (author)|Anthony Browne]] wrote and illustrated another juvenile adaptation called ''Anthony Browne's King Kong'' in 1994. Credited as "From the Story Conceived by Edgar Wallace & Merian C. Cooper", the book was published by the [[Turner Publishing Company]]. It was re-released as a paperback in the U.K in 2005 by [[Transworld Publishers|Picture Corgi]]. Blackstone Audio produced an audio recording of the book in 2005 narrated by Stefan Rudnicki, while StarWarp Concepts released an Ebook version complete with 6 new illustrations from pulp-comic artist Paul Tuma in 2017.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.flickeringmyth.com/2017/02/king-kong-unleashed-by-starwarp-concepts/ |title=King Kong unleashed by StarWarp Concepts |website=flickeringmyth.com |date=February 21, 2017 |access-date=February 23, 2017 }}{{Dead link|date=September 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

Outside of the novelization, the film was serialized in a pulp magazine. In 1933, ''Mystery'' magazine published a ''King Kong'' [[Serial (literature)|serial]] under the byline of Edgar Wallace,<ref>{{cite web |title=King Kong |url=http://www.tebeosfera.com/obras/series/king_kong_cooper_wallace_rko_1933.html |language=es |website=tebeosfera.com |access-date=August 11, 2016 |archive-date=August 16, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160816131055/http://www.tebeosfera.com/obras/series/king_kong_cooper_wallace_rko_1933.html |url-status=live}}</ref> and written by Walter F. Ripperger.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://img101.xooimage.com/files/4/5/2/mystery-magazine-46165a2.jpg|title=Mystery Magazine scan|access-date=June 9, 2019|archive-date=February 2, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170202001000/http://img101.xooimage.com/files/4/5/2/mystery-magazine-46165a2.jpg|url-status=live}}</ref> This serialization was published in two parts in the February and March issues of the magazine.

In the U.K, the film was serialized in two different pulps, both on October 28, 1933: in the juvenile ''Boys Magazine'' (Vol. 23, No. 608),<ref>[http://oi59.tinypic.com/kbahcl.jpg Boy's Magazine Cover] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305011421/http://oi59.tinypic.com/kbahcl.jpg |date=March 5, 2016}} (jpg image).tinypic.us</ref> where the serialization was uncredited, and in that month's issue of ''Cinema Weekly'' where it was credited to Edgar Wallace and written by Draycott Montagu Dell (1888–1940). This short story adaptation would later appear in the [[Peter Haining (author)|Peter Haining]] book called ''Movie Monsters'' in 1988, published by Severn House in the U.K. The novel was serialized in the [[Daily Herald (United Kingdom)|London Daily Herald]] by H. Kingsley Long as well. The serialization was first published in April 1933 and ran 37 installments.

In 1973, [[Philip Jose Farmer]] wrote a short story sequel to the Lovelace novelization called ''After King Kong Fell'' that was published in ''OMEGA: a collection of original science fiction stories''.<ref name="Philip Jose Farmer International Bibliography">{{cite web| title=After King Kong Fell| url=https://www.philipjosefarmer.com/ShoFic/SFakkf.htm| access-date=June 8, 2019| archive-date=May 8, 2021| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210508051225/http://philipjosefarmer.com/ShoFic/SFakkf.htm| url-status=live}}</ref>

In 1977, a novelization of the 1976 remake of ''King Kong'' was published by [[Ace Books]]. This novelization was called ''The Dino De Laurentiis Production of King Kong'' and was simply the 1976 Lorenzo Semple Jr. script published in book form. The cover was done by [[Frank Frazetta]].

To coincide with the 2005 remake of ''King Kong'', various books were released to tie into the film. A novelization was written by [[Christopher Golden]] based on the screenplay by Fran Walsh, Philippa Boyens, and [[Peter Jackson]]. Matt Costello wrote an official prequel to the film called ''King Kong: The Island of the Skull''. These books were published by [[Pocket Books]]. Various illustrated juvenile books were published, as well, by [[HarperCollins|Harper Books]]: ''Kong's Kingdom'' was written by Julia Simon-Kerr; ''Meet Kong and Ann'' and ''Journey to Skull Island'' were written by Jennifer Franz; ''Escape from Skull Island'' and ''Kong: The Eighth Wonder of the World—Junior Novel'' were written by Laura J. Burns; ''The Search for Kong'' was written by Catherine Hapka; and finally, a Deluxe Sound Storybook of ''Kong: The Eighth Wonder of the World'' was written by Don Curry. [[Weta Workshop]] released a collection of concept art from the film entitled ''The World of Kong: A Natural History of Skull Island'' that was published by Pocket Books. The book was written and designed to resemble and read like an actual nature guide and historical record.

In 2005, Ibooks, Inc., published an unofficial book featuring King Kong called ''Kong Reborn'', by [[Russell Blackford]].

Starting in 1996, artist/writer Joe DeVito began working with the Merian C. Cooper estate to write and/or illustrate various books based on the King Kong character. The first of these was an origin story labeled as an authorized sequel/prequel to the 1932 novelization of ''King Kong'' called ''Kong: King of Skull Island''. This illustrated hardcover novel was published in 2004 by [[DH Press]] and featured a story DeVito co-wrote with [[Brad Strickland]] and John Michlig. It also included an introduction by [[Ray Harryhausen]]. A large paperback edition was then released in 2005, with extra pages at the end of the book. A CD audiobook narrated by Joey D'Auria was released by RadioArchives as well, and an interactive two-part app was released in 2011 and 2013, respectively, by Copyright 1957 LLC. In 2005, DeVito and Strickland co-wrote another book together called ''Merian C. Cooper's King Kong'' for the Merian C. Cooper estate. This book was published by [[St. Martin's Press]]. It was a full rewrite of the original 1932 novelization, which updates the language and paleontology and adds five new chapters. Some additional elements and characters tie into ''Kong: King of Skull Island'', enabling the two separate books to form a continuous storyline.
In 2013, the first of two books featuring crossovers with pulp heroes was published. To coincide with the 80th anniversary of both King Kong and [[Doc Savage]], [[Altus Press]] published ''Doc Savage: Skull Island'' in both softcover and hardcover editions. This officially sanctioned book was written by [[Will Murray]] and based on concepts by DeVito.<ref>{{cite press release | first= Matthew | last= Moring | title= Press Release: Doc Savage and King Kong Coming in March | url= http://www.altuspress.com/2013/01/press-release-doc-savage-and-king-kong-coming-in-march/ | publisher= [[Altus Press]] | date= January 29, 2012 | access-date= February 24, 2013 | url-status= dead | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130218100701/http://www.altuspress.com/2013/01/press-release-doc-savage-and-king-kong-coming-in-march/ | archive-date= February 18, 2013 | df= mdy-all}}</ref> In 2016, Altus Press published the other crossover book, this time featuring a meeting between King Kong and [[Tarzan]]. The novel, called ''[[King Kong vs. Tarzan]]'', was once again written by Will Murray and featured artwork by DeVito.<ref name="Forces of Geek">{{cite web | title= The Ultimate Rumble in the Jungle!: King Kong Vs. Tarzan Arrives This Summer! | url= http://www.forcesofgeek.com/2016/03/the-ultimate-rumble-in-jungle-king-kong.html | work= Forces of Geek | date= March 9, 2016 | access-date= March 9, 2016 | archive-date= March 10, 2016 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160310013512/http://www.forcesofgeek.com/2016/03/the-ultimate-rumble-in-jungle-king-kong.html | url-status= dead}}</ref> In 2017, a new book featuring another origin story, written and illustrated by DeVito, was released, called ''King Kong of Skull Island''.<ref name="forcesofgeek.com">{{cite web | title= Fog! Chats With Author/Artist Joe DeVito About His 'King Kong of Skull Island' Kickstarter | url= http://www.forcesofgeek.com/2017/03/fog-chats-with-authorartist-joe-devito-about-his-king-kong-of-skull-island-kickstarter.html | work= Forces of Geek | date= March 3, 2017 | access-date= March 16, 2017 | archive-date= March 17, 2017 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170317054857/http://www.forcesofgeek.com/2017/03/fog-chats-with-authorartist-joe-devito-about-his-king-kong-of-skull-island-kickstarter.html | url-status= live}}</ref> Expanded versions of the book titled ''King Kong Skull Island: Exodus'' and ''King Kong Skull Island: The Wall'' were released by Markosia in 2020.<ref>{{cite web | title= kongskullisland.com | url= https://www.kongskullisland.com/ | access-date= June 2, 2020 | archive-date= June 27, 2020 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200627094943/http://www.kongskullisland.com/ | url-status= live}}</ref>

In March 2017, to coincide with the release of ''[[Kong: Skull Island]]'', [[Titan Books]] released a novelization of the film written by Tim Lebbon and a hardcover book ''The Art and Making of Kong: Skull Island'' by Simon Ward.<ref>{{cite web | title= Official Kong: Skull Island Art Book and Movie Novelization announced! | url= http://www.scified.com/news/official-kong-skull-island-art-book-and-movie-novelization-announced | work= Scified.com | date= September 1, 2016 | access-date= December 2, 2016 | archive-date= December 3, 2016 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20161203060357/http://www.scified.com/news/official-kong-skull-island-art-book-and-movie-novelization-announced | url-status= live}}</ref>

In 2021, to coincide with the release of ''[[Godzilla vs. Kong]]'', various tie-in books were released. On March 30, [[Legendary Comics]] released a graphic prequel novel called ''Kingdom Kong'' written by Marie Anello,<ref>{{cite web| title= Legendary Comics Unleashes Cover Art for Upcoming 'Godzilla vs. Kong' Graphic Novel Prequels| url= https://bloody-disgusting.com/comics/3653659/legendary-comics-unleashes-cover-art-upcoming-godzilla-vs-kong-graphic-novel-prequels/| work= Bloodydisgusting.com| date= February 25, 2021| accessdate= February 26, 2021| archive-date= February 25, 2021| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210225233937/https://bloody-disgusting.com/comics/3653659/legendary-comics-unleashes-cover-art-upcoming-godzilla-vs-kong-graphic-novel-prequels/| url-status= live}}</ref> as well as a children's picture book called ''Kong and Me'' written by Kiki Thorpe and illustrated by Nidhi Chanani.<ref>{{cite web | title= Legendary's MonsterVerse Heads into the World of Children's Books With 'Kong and Me' | url= https://bloody-disgusting.com/books/3651556/legendarys-monsterverse-heads-world-childrens-books-kong/ | work= Bloodydisgusting.com | date= February 10, 2021 | accessdate= February 10, 2021 | archive-date= February 10, 2021 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210210160153/https://bloody-disgusting.com/books/3651556/legendarys-monsterverse-heads-world-childrens-books-kong/ | url-status= live}}</ref> 
On April 6, Titan Books released ''Godzilla vs. Kong: The Official Movie Novelization'' written by Greg Keyes.<ref>{{cite web| title='Godzilla vs. Kong': The Official Movie Novelization Being Unleashed in April| url=https://bloody-disgusting.com/books/3652271/godzilla-vs-kong-official-movie-novelization-unleashed-april/| work=Bloodydisgusting.com| date=February 16, 2021| accessdate=February 16, 2021| archive-date=February 16, 2021| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210216154426/https://bloody-disgusting.com/books/3652271/godzilla-vs-kong-official-movie-novelization-unleashed-april/| url-status=live}}</ref> Insight Editions released a children's board book called ''Godzilla vs. Kong: Sometimes Friends Fight (But They Always Make Up)'' written by Carol Herring,<ref>{{cite web| title= 'Godzilla vs. Kong: Sometimes Friends Fight': Preview of Upcoming Children's Book Will Warm Your Heart| url= https://bloody-disgusting.com/books/3652149/godzilla-vs-kong-sometimes-friends-fight-preview-upcoming-childrens-book-will-warm-heart/| work= Bloodydisgusting.com| date= February 15, 2021| accessdate= February 15, 2021| archive-date= February 15, 2021| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210215215212/https://bloody-disgusting.com/books/3652149/godzilla-vs-kong-sometimes-friends-fight-preview-upcoming-childrens-book-will-warm-heart/| url-status= live}}</ref> and on May 21, also released ''Godzilla vs. Kong: One Will Fall, The Art of the Ultimate Battle Royale'' written by Daniel Wallace.<ref>{{cite web | title= Godzilla vs Kong News Roundup | url= https://www.tohokingdom.com/blog/godzilla-vs-kong-news-roundup/ | work= tohokingdom.com | date= February 11, 2021 | accessdate= February 12, 2021 | archive-date= February 24, 2021 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210224040533/https://www.tohokingdom.com/blog/godzilla-vs-kong-news-roundup/ | url-status= live}}</ref>

In April 2024, to coincide with the release of ''[[Godzilla x Kong: The New Empire]]'', [[Titan Books]] released a novelization of the film written by Greg Keyes.<ref>{{cite web | title= Official 'Godzilla x Kong: The New Empire' Novelization Releasing in April | url= https://bloody-disgusting.com/books/3801275/official-godzilla-x-kong-the-new-empire-novelization-releasing-in-april/ | work= Bloody Disgusting | date= February 26, 2024 | access-date= February 27, 2024}}</ref>

===Theme park rides===
[[File:Skull Island (37281017260).jpg|thumb|''Skull Island: Reign of Kong'' at [[Universal's Islands of Adventure]]]]
Universal Studios has had popular King Kong attractions at [[Universal Studios Hollywood]] in [[Universal City, California]] and [[Universal Orlando]] Resort in [[Orlando, Florida]].

The first King Kong attraction was called ''[[King Kong Encounter]]'' and was a part of the [[Studio Tour]] at Universal Studios Hollywood. Based upon the 1976 film ''King Kong'', the tour took the guests in the world of 1976 New York City, where Kong was seen wreaking havoc on the city. It was opened on June 14, 1986, and was destroyed on June 1, 2008, in a major fire. Universal opened a replacement 3D King Kong ride called ''[[King Kong: 360 3-D]]'' that opened on July 1, 2010, based upon [[Peter Jackson]]'s 2005 film ''King Kong''.<ref name="latimes1">{{cite news |last=Mart |first=Hugo |url=https://www.latimes.com/business/la-fi-kong27-2010jan27,0,493042.story |title=&#124; King Kong to rejoin Universal tours in 3-D |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=January 27, 2010 |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=January 30, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100130175723/http://www.latimes.com/business/la-fi-kong27-2010jan27,0,493042.story |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.dreadcentral.com/news/36239/badass-new-teaser-promo-universals-new-king-kong-ride |title=Badass New Teaser Promo for Universal's New King Kong Ride |publisher=Dreadcentral.com |access-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-date=March 8, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100308001536/http://www.dreadcentral.com/news/36239/badass-new-teaser-promo-universals-new-king-kong-ride |url-status=live}}</ref>

A second more elaborate ride was constructed at [[Universal Studios Florida]] on June 7, 1990, called ''[[Kongfrontation]]''. The ride featured a stand-alone extended version of ''King Kong Encounter'' and pinned guests escaping on the [[Roosevelt Island Tramway]] from Kong, who was rampaging across New York City. The ride was closed down on September 8, 2002, and was replaced with ''[[Revenge of the Mummy]]'' on May 21, 2004.

A new King Kong attraction titled ''[[Skull Island: Reign of Kong]]'' opened at [[Islands of Adventure]] on July 13, 2016,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://blog.universalorlando.com/whats-new/reign-of-kong/ |title=King Kong Roars to Life in Groundbreaking new Attraction |publisher=blog.universalorlando.com |access-date=May 6, 2015 |archive-date=September 27, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150927161615/http://blog.universalorlando.com/whats-new/reign-of-kong/ |url-status=live}}</ref> making it the first King Kong themed ride in Orlando since Kongfrontation closed down 14 years earlier at Universal Studios Florida.

A ride based on the [[Monsterverse]] titled ''Kong x Godzilla: The Ride'' opened at the [[Lotte World]] Adventure in Seoul, South Korea in December 2025.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.licenseglobal.com/location-based-entertainment/kong-godzilla-coming-to-lotte-world-adventure-in-world-s-first-monsterverse-theme-park-ride |title=Kong, Godzilla Coming to Lotte World Adventure in World's First Monsterverse Theme Park Ride |publisher=licenseglobal.com |access-date=May 21, 2025}}</ref>

===Musical===
{{Further|King Kong (2013 musical)}}
A musical adaptation of the story (endorsed by Merian C. Cooper's estate)<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.playbill.com/article/king-kong-musical-aiming-for-broadway-in-2013-creative-team-announced-com-171812|title=King Kong Musical Aiming for Broadway in 2013; Creative Team Announced|work=Playbill|date=September 16, 2010|access-date=November 27, 2018|archive-date=November 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181129012816/http://www.playbill.com/article/king-kong-musical-aiming-for-broadway-in-2013-creative-team-announced-com-171812|url-status=live}}</ref> was staged in [[Melbourne]] at the Regent Theatre. The show premiered on June 15, 2013, as ''King Kong: The Eighth Wonder of The World'', with music by Marius De Vries. The musical then premiered on Broadway on November 8, 2018, at the [[Broadway Theatre (53rd Street)|Broadway Theatre]] as ''King Kong: Alive on Broadway''. The creative team included book writer Jack Thorne, director-choreographer Drew McOnie, and Australian songwriter Eddie Perfect, who replace the former creatives.<ref>Lefkowitz, Andy. [http://www.broadway.com/buzz/188645/king-kong-musical-will-stomp-onto-broadway-in-2018/ "King Kong Musical Will Stomp onto Broadway in 2018"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180930033759/https://www.broadway.com/buzz/188645/king-kong-musical-will-stomp-onto-broadway-in-2018/ |date=September 30, 2018}} broadway.com, May 18, 2017</ref>

The huge King Kong puppet was created by Global Creature Technology.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://kingkongliveonstage.com/ |title=King Kong Live on Stage at the Regent Theatre, Melbourne |website=Kingkongliveonstage.com |date=March 17, 2014 |access-date=January 28, 2015 |archive-date=January 10, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160110164337/http://kingkongliveonstage.com/ |url-status=dead}}</ref> The puppet stands 20 feet tall and weighs 2,400 pounds. It is operated by a large rig with 10 onstage puppeteers,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://ew.com/theater/2018/11/05/king-kong-musical-broadway-puppet//|title=The new King of Broadway: Meet the King Kong musical's 2,000-pound puppet|work=EntertainmentWeekly|date=November 5, 2018|access-date=November 25, 2018|archive-date=November 25, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181125204404/https://ew.com/theater/2018/11/05/king-kong-musical-broadway-puppet//|url-status=live}}</ref> and features an array of microprocessors and tiny motors that power nuanced movements in the facial features.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.syfy.com/king-kong-broadway-puppet-built|title=Inside the one-ton, history-making King Kong Broadway musical|work=SyFy|date=October 8, 2018|access-date=December 14, 2018|archive-date=December 15, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181215023552/https://www.syfy.com/king-kong-broadway-puppet-built|url-status=live}}</ref> According to Sonny Tilders, who designed the Fiberglas and steel puppet for Global Creatures Company, "It's the most sophisticated marionette puppet ever made". Tilders also stated that Kong is built in layers, and is "quite similar to genuine anatomy". Over the steel skeleton, the body shell is a mixture of hard Fiberglas, enforced inflatables, high-pressure inflatables, and bags full of styrene beans that stretch and contort like muscles. Tilders said that they wanted to create the sense that Kong is a moving sculpture.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.newsday.com/entertainment/theater/king-kong-broadway-v24445|title='King Kong': The beauty of the beast|work=Newsday|date=October 31, 2018|access-date=November 25, 2018|archive-date=November 25, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181125204512/https://www.newsday.com/entertainment/theater/king-kong-broadway-1.22652511|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Other appearances===
* The 1996 [[List of IMAX films|IMAX]] [[Documentary film|documentary]] ''[[Special Effects: Anything Can Happen]]'' featured a short remake of the original King Kong's ending set in Los Angeles.<ref>{{Cite web |title=NOVA Online/Special Effects/All About Special Effects/Burtt Interview #2 |url=https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/specialfx/effects/director2.html |access-date=2026-08-20 |website=www.pbs.org}}</ref> The remade scenes were the subject of its own documentary entitled ''Behind the Scenes with King Kong in Special Effects.''<ref>{{Cite web |title=Behind the Scenes with King Kong in Special Effects (1996) {{!}} Film |url=https://telescopefilm.com/films/473825-behind-the-scenes-with-king-kong-in-special-effects |access-date=2026-08-20 |website=Telescope Film |language=en-US}}</ref>
King Kong had three cameos in Warner Bros films:
* In the 2017 film ''[[The Lego Batman Movie]]'', King Kong (voiced by [[Seth Green]]) appears as an inmate of the [[Phantom Zone]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dreadcentral.com/news/212909/king-kong-will-appear-lego-batman-movie/|title=King Kong Will Appear in the Lego Batman Movie|first=David|last=Gelmini|date=February 3, 2017|access-date=January 20, 2021|work=[[DreadCentral]]|publisher=[[Dreadcentral.com]]|archive-date=January 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128153957/https://www.dreadcentral.com/news/212909/king-kong-will-appear-lego-batman-movie/|url-status=live}}</ref> He is among the Phantom Zone inmates that the [[Joker (character)|Joker]] releases in order to take over [[Gotham City]]. During the Joker's campaign, King Kong destroys one of the towers that [[Batman]] and the others hide in. With help from [[Dick Grayson|Robin]], [[Barbara Gordon|Batgirl]], [[Alfred Pennyworth]], and his enemies, Batman is able to defeat King Kong and send him and his fellow Phantom Zone inmates back to the Phantom Zone.
* In the 2018 film ''[[Ready Player One (film)|Ready Player One]]'' (based on the [[Ready Player One|book of the same name]]), King Kong appears as one of the hazards of the OASIS racetrack.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dreadcentral.com/news/262298/catch-chuckys-cameo-trailer-steven-spielbergs-new-movie/|title=Did You Catch Chucky and King Kong's Cameo in the Trailer For Steven Spielberg's New Movie?|first=Mike|last=Sprague|date=December 12, 2017|access-date=December 13, 2017|work=[[DreadCentral]]|publisher=[[Dreadcentral.com]]|archive-date=December 13, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171213012352/http://www.dreadcentral.com/news/262298/catch-chuckys-cameo-trailer-steven-spielbergs-new-movie/|url-status=live}}</ref>
* In the 2021 film ''[[Space Jam: A New Legacy]]'', King Kong makes a cameo as one of the many spectators to a basketball game between the Tune Squad and the Goon Squad.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.screengeek.net/2021/04/03/space-jam-a-new-legacy-trailer-pennywise-king-kong-joker/|title='Space Jam: A New Legacy' Shows Off Pennywise, King Kong, And More|first=Jacob|last=Dressler|date=April 3, 2021|access-date=June 9, 2021|archive-date=June 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210609145400/https://www.screengeek.net/2021/04/03/space-jam-a-new-legacy-trailer-pennywise-king-kong-joker/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Other references===
*King Kong, in name only, was referenced by [[Ian Malcolm (character)|Ian Malcolm]] as a sarcastic remark toward the gates of ''[[Jurassic Park (film)|Jurassic Park]]'' in the titular 1993 film.
*A frame of King Kong in the 1933 film can be seen for a split second during the 2008 monster film ''[[Cloverfield]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://bloody-disgusting.com/news/3425598/ever-spot-hidden-king-kong-tribute-cloverfield/|title=Did You Ever Spot Hidden King Kong Tribute in 'Cloverfield'?|first=John|last=Squires|date=February 22, 2017|access-date=January 22, 2021|archive-date=November 12, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112020104/https://bloody-disgusting.com/news/3425598/ever-spot-hidden-king-kong-tribute-cloverfield/|url-status=live}}</ref>
*Both King Kong and Skull Island were referenced and made multiple appearances in the 2019 film ''[[Godzilla: King of the Monsters (2019 film)|Godzilla: King of the Monsters]]''.

===King Kong in the name===
{{more citations needed section|date=September 2020}}
[[File:kingkongedo38.jpg|frame|A publicity still of Isamu Yamaguchi playing King Kong on stage from ''[[Wasei Kingu Kongu]]'']]
There were other unofficial movies to have borne the "King Kong" name:
* A lost silent Japanese short, {{nihongo|''[[Wasei Kingu Kongu|Japanese King Kong]]''|和製キングコング|Wasei Kingu Kongu}}, directed by [[Torajiro Saito]], featuring an all-Japanese cast and produced by the [[Shochiku]] company, was released in 1933. The plot revolves around a down-on-his-luck man who plays the King Kong character in a vaudeville theater to earn money to woo a girl he likes. The film does not actually involve King Kong ''per se''.
* {{nihongo|''[[The King Kong That Appeared in Edo]]''|江戸に現れたキングコング|Edo ni Arawareta Kingu Kongu}}: a lost two-part silent Japanese period piece that was produced by a company called Zensho Kinema in 1938. The film revolves around kidnapping and revenge amongst the characters. The "King Kong" in this film is a trained ape (that looks more like a Yeti) who is used to kidnap one of the characters. Judging by the plot synopsis presented by periodicals at the time, the "King Kong" is regular-sized and is only depicted as gigantic on the advertisements for promotional purposes.<ref>{{cite book |author=高槻真樹 (Maki Takatsuki) |title=戦前日本ＳＦ映画創世記 ゴジラは何でできているか (Senzen Nihon SF Eiga Souseiki) |publisher=河出書房新社 (Kawadeshobo Shinsha publishing) |year=2014 |pages=183–188 |language=ja}}</ref>
* The 1959 Hong Kong film ''King Kong's Adventures in the Heavenly Palace'' (猩猩王大鬧天宮), which features a normal-sized gorilla.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://movie.douban.com/subject/25820643/|title=猩猩王大闹天宫 (豆瓣)|website=movie.douban.com|access-date=August 10, 2021|archive-date=October 11, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201011133652/https://movie.douban.com/subject/25820643/|url-status=live}}</ref>
* The Hindi films ''King Kong'' (1962) and ''Tarzan and King Kong'' (1965), which feature the professional wrestler [[Emile Czaja|King Kong]] and have nothing to do with the famous movie monster, although the latter film features a normal-sized gorilla.
* The 1968 Italian film ''[[Kong Island]]'' (''Eva, la Venere selvaggia'', {{lit|Eve, the wild Venus}}), which was advertised in the U.S. as ''[[Kong Island|King of Kong Island]]''. Despite the American title, the film features normal-sized gorillas and takes place in Africa.
* The 1981 Mexican film ''Las Muñecas Del King Kong'' (''The Dolls of King Kong''), which features exotic jungle girls. The "King Kong" in the film is simply a giant ape statue on top of a building.

===Related films===
{{more citations needed section|date=September 2020}}
* The premise of a giant gorilla brought to the United States for entertainment purposes, and subsequently wreaking havoc, was recycled in ''[[Mighty Joe Young (1949 film)|Mighty Joe Young]]'' (1949), through the same studio and with much of the same principal talent as the 1933 original. It was [[Mighty Joe Young (1998 film)|remade in 1998]].
* ''King Kong'' bears some similarities with an earlier effort by special effects head [[Willis H. O'Brien]], ''[[The Lost World (1925 film)|The Lost World]]'' (1925), in which [[dinosaur]]s are found living on an isolated [[plateau]]. It was based on Sir [[Arthur Conan Doyle]]'s [[The Lost World (Doyle novel)|novel of the same name]].
* ''Banglar King Kong'' – an unofficial [[Bangladesh]]i musical based on the King Kong story and directed by Iftekar Jahan. The film uses large amounts of stock footage from the films ''[[King Kong (1976 film)|King Kong]]'' and ''[[Mighty Joe Young (1998 film)|Mighty Joe Young]]'', as well as [[National Geographic Society|National Geographic]] documentaries, and premiered in June 2010 in the Purnima Cinema Hall in [[Dhaka]].<ref>[http://roberthood.net/blog/index.php/2010/06/11/the-ongoing-travels-of-king-kong/ The Ongoing Travels of King Kong] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190609162347/http://roberthood.net/blog/index.php/2010/06/11/the-ongoing-travels-of-king-kong/ |date=June 9, 2019}}. Roberthood.net (June 11, 2010). Retrieved on December 21, 2012.</ref>
* Other similar giant ape films include:
** The 1949 American comedy film ''[[Africa Screams]]'', where a giant ape briefly appears.
** The 1961 British film ''[[Konga (film)|Konga]]'', where a chimpanzee is enlarged after being fed a growth serum by a deranged scientist and attacks [[London]].
** The 1969 American film ''[[The Mighty Gorga]]'', which features a circus owner's quest to capture a giant gorilla in an African jungle. Unlike King Kong, Gorga remains in Africa.
** The 1976 [[Korea]]n [[3-D film|3D]] film ''[[Ape (1976 film)|Ape]]'',<ref>[https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0074148/ Ape (1976)] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222060920/https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0074148/ |date=December 22, 2019}}. imdb.com</ref> where a giant ape runs amok in [[Seoul]], [[South Korea]].
** The 1976 British film ''[[Queen Kong]]'', a film that parodies ''King Kong'' with a gender reversal between the giant ape and the object of the ape's affection.
** The 1977 [[Hong Kong]] film ''[[The Mighty Peking Man]]'', featuring a huge [[Bigfoot]]/ape-like creature that attacks Hong Kong after it was brought to Hong Kong from its territory somewhere in India near the Himalayas.
** The 1977 [[Italy|Italian]] film ''[[Yeti: Giant of the 20th Century]]'', featuring a giant [[Yeti|abominable snowman]] running amok in Ontario after a millionaire industrialist thaws it out of a block of ice.
** The 2005 [[The Asylum]] film ''[[King of the Lost World]]'' is a [[mockbuster]] of Jackson's ''King Kong'', loosely based on the aforementioned ''The Lost World''.
** The 2018 American film ''[[Rampage (2018 film)|Rampage]]'', featuring a giant [[Albinism|albino]] [[western lowland gorilla]] named George. The film is based on the 1986 [[arcade game]] ''[[Rampage (video game)|Rampage]]'', which itself is a video game parody of giant monster films.

